ng_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module2", "page_count": 27, "document_sha256": "4f676a58615653683b36cf6b15779a8a82614c1ee8a021487d2e3c547bbe1cc7", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_2_module_3", "number": 3, "title": "Pheochromocytoma: Diagnosis, Perioperative Optimization, and Intraoperative Anesthetic Management", "short_title": "Pheochromocytoma", "credits": 2.0, "pharmacology_credits": 1.5, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Define pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma (PPGL), describe the catecholamine-driven pathophysiology, and explain why chronic vasoconstriction, catecholamine cardiomyopathy, and volume contraction make these tumors a perioperative high-risk state for the CRNA.", "Identify the clinical features, epidemiology, and hereditary syndromes (MEN2, VHL, NF1, SDHx, and other 20+ known susceptibility genes) that should raise suspicion for PPGL, and describe when biochemical and genetic screening are indicated.", "Describe the recommended biochemical (plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines) and imaging (CT/MRI plus functional imaging when indicated) approaches used to confirm and localize PPGL.", "Summarize definitive treatment options \u2014 including minimally invasive and open adrenalectomy, expanding robotic experience, and emerging systemic therapies for metastatic disease (belzutifan HIF-2\u03b1 inhibition, MIBG-based therapy, targeted kinase inhibitors) \u2014 and explain the goals, timing, and endpoints of preoperative medical optimization.", "Contrast the pharmacology, dosing, and clinical trade-offs of phenoxybenzamine (nonselective) versus doxazosin/prazosin/terazosin (selective alpha-1) blockade in light of the PRESCRIPT randomized trial (Buitenwerf 2020) and the Vorselaars 2022 crossover phenylephrine-test study; explain when beta blockade, calcium channel blockers, metyrosine, and magnesium sulfate are added to the preoperative regimen.", "Apply evidence-based anesthetic management principles for the preoperative, intraoperative, and immediate postoperative phases \u2014 including invasive monitoring, vascular access, choice of induction and maintenance agents, avoidance of catecholamine-releasing or histamine-releasing drugs, and phase-specific readiness at induction, insufflation, tumor manipulation, and adrenal vein ligation.", "Anticipate and manage the two dominant hemodynamic emergencies of PPGL resection: catecholamine-driven hypertensive crisis (sodium nitroprusside, nicardipine, clevidipine, phentolamine, nitroglycerin, esmolol, magnesium sulfate) and post-resection vasodilatory hypotension (norepinephrine, phenylephrine, vasopressin, epinephrine, judicious volume) \u2014 including choice, sequencing, and endpoint monitoring.", "Recognize the intraoperative presentation of an undiagnosed pheochromocytoma during a nonadrenal case, execute the CRNA immediate-action bundle (deepen anesthesia, invasive monitoring, short-acting vasodilators, cautious beta blockade, magnesium adjunct, consider aborting elective surgery), and plan ICU-level postoperative care and endocrine referral.", "Identify common postoperative complications \u2014 recurrent hypertension, arrhythmias, myocardial ischemia, prolonged vasopressor requirement, hypoglycemia from rebound insulin, adrenal insufficiency in bilateral cases \u2014 and describe long-term follow-up including lifelong biochemical surveillance and hereditary counseling per the Endocrine Society guideline (Lenders 2014, endorsed through 2025)."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 