and obstetric care.", "Evaluate the risks, prerequisites, and factor-activity thresholds for neuraxial and peripheral regional techniques in patients with suspected or confirmed VWD, including the guideline factor range (50\u2013150%) for neuraxial placement, the more conservative institutional threshold (VWF \u2265100%) many programs adopt for labor epidural placement, and the deep-versus-superficial block framework.", "Apply postoperative monitoring and rescue strategies for delayed bleeding, progressive FVIII accumulation with repeated dosing of FVIII-containing concentrates, venous thromboembolism risk, and opioid-sparing multimodal pain management that avoids platelet-inhibiting NSAIDs when alternatives exist.", "Recognize situations that warrant urgent hematology consultation, higher-acuity postoperative care, or modification of the proposed procedure or anesthetic technique \u2014 including undiagnosed VWD presenting as unexpected surgical hemorrhage, acquired VWS in aortic stenosis (Heyde syndrome), LVAD support, or lymphoproliferative disease, and the pregnant VWD patient at term."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module4", "page_count": 34, "document_sha256": "0e8cbf485fabed76af9ffc253cfa00db76461a7f0731cb0c1c409de70cccc15d", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_2_module_5", "number": 5, "title": "Serotonin Syndrome: Perioperative Recognition, Prevention, and Management", "short_title": "Serotonin Syndrome", "credits": 2.0, "pharmacology_credits": 1.0, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Define serotonin syndrome (serotonin toxicity), describe its clinical spectrum from mild to life-threatening, and explain why it is a high-priority perioperative diagnosis for the CRNA.", "Describe the pathophysiology of serotonin excess, including the roles of 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors, mechanisms of increased synaptic serotonin (reuptake inhibition, monoamine oxidase inhibition, direct agonism, increased release, and impaired clearance), and the pharmacokinetic reasons that fluoxetine and other long-half-life agents may prolong toxicity.", "Identify the outpatient medication classes (MAOIs, SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs, trazodone, buspirone, lithium, St. John's wort, dextromethorphan, MDMA) and the perioperative agents (methylene blue, linezolid, meperidine, tramadol, methadone, fentanyl, metoclopramide, ondansetron) that precipitate serotonin syndrome, and grade the relative risk of each class in monotherapy versus combination.", "Recognize the clinical triad of altered mental status, autonomic instability, and neuromuscular hyperactivity, with particular attention to inducible clonus, spontaneous clonus, ocular clonus, and lower-extremity hyperreflexia as the highest-yield examination findings.", "Apply the Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria at the bedside and differentiate serotonin syndrome from malignant hyperthermia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, anticholinergic toxicity, thyroid storm, sepsis, stimulant intoxication, and withdrawal syndromes.", "Outline severity-based management for mild, moderate, and severe serotonin syndrome \u2014 including discontinuation of offending agents, benzodiazepine sedation, active external cooling, avoidance of antipyretics, avoidance of succinylcholine when hyperkalemia or rhabdomyolysis is a concern, and airway control with nondepolarizing paralysis for refractory rigidity or hyperthermia.", "Evaluate the current low-quality evidence for cyproheptadine, describe when it may be considered as adjunctive therapy, and explain why supportive care remains the foundation of treatment per the 2024 Chiew and Isbister BJCP review.", "Incorporate prevention strategies into everyday CRNA practice \u2014 including a focused medication reconciliation for serotonergic agents, avoidance of high-risk perioperative combinations (SSRI/SNRI + methylene blue, MAOI + meperidine, SSRI + tramadol), preferential use of morphine or hydromorphone in high-risk patients, and structured handoff communication in the PACU and ICU."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module5", "page_count": 30, "document_sha256": "db4e808c371b1517b4ca28fbc08fa7c9af54bea7b6443fef24c47b07f2a7f0e3", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_2_module_6", "number": 6, "title": "Fire Safety and Fire Prevention in the Operating Room", "short_title": "OR Fire Safety", "credits": 1.5, "pharmacology_credits": 0.0, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Describe the three elements of the surgical fire triad \u2014 oxidizer, ignition source, and fuel \u2014 and explain how each is generated or controlled by members of the perioperative team.", "Identify patient, procedure, and environmental factors that elevate the risk of an operating room fire, including head/face/neck/upper-chest surgery, MAC with open oxygen delivery, airway procedures, and electrocautery use near oxidizer-enriched fields.", "Recognize the common flammable liquids and fuel sources encountered in the OR, including alcohol-based skin antiseptics (ChloraPrep, DuraPrep, 3M SoluPrep), alcohol-containing iodophors, tinctures, adhesive removers, drapes, sponges, tracheal tubes, and patient hair.", "Apply evidence-based prevention strategies for oxygen management (target FiO2 \u226430% by open delivery in high-risk cases, secure the airway when higher concentrations are needed, avoid nitrous oxide, use air-oxygen blends, allow oxygen to dissipate before ignition source activation) and prep-solution management (verify complete drying, avoid pooling in hair/skin folds/linens, use appropriate applicator size).", "Explain the CRNA's role in the pre-procedure fire risk assessment and surgical time-out, including announcing changes in oxygen concentration, confirming prep-dry status, and coordinating with the surgeon before electrocautery or laser activation.", "Outline the immediate coordinated response to a fire on the patient, in the airway, or in the room \u2014 including the CRNA's priority action of discontinuing oxygen and nitrous oxide, and the correct sequence of the seven-step surgical fire response algorithm within the first 60 seconds.", "Distinguish the surgical fire response (announce \u2192 stop oxidizer \u2192 stop ignition \u2192 remove fuel \u2192 extinguish \u2192 re-establish ventilation \u2192 report) from the RACE facility fire model and the PASS mnemonic for extinguisher use, and explain when each applies.", "Discuss post-event management, including bronchoscopy after airway fires, burn and inhalation-injury assessment, event documentation, institutional reporting, structured debriefing, and systems improvement through drills and simulation."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module6", "page_count": 24, "document_sha256": "802fb540d56bfee05c828f3c9680f64471c1a7c9d45b24f7ca5f0394d85e4f46", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_2_module_7", "number": 7, "title": "Negative Pressure Pulmonary Edema (NPPE)", "short_title": "Negative Pressure Pulmonary Edema", "credits": 2.0, "pharmacology_credits": 1.0, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Define negative pressure pulmonary edema (NPPE), distinguish it from cardiogenic pulmonary edema, and place it within the differential diagnosis of acute postoperative hypoxemia.", "Explain the hydrostatic and permeability mechanisms by which markedly negative intrathoracic pressure produces alveolar and interstitial edema after upper airway obstruction, including the roles of increased venous return, elevated pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, sympathetic surge, and transient alveolar-capillary membrane stress.", "Differentiate Type I NPPE (acute obstruction \u2014 for example, laryngospasm after extubation) from Type II NPPE (after relief of chronic upper airway obstruction) and identify perioperative risk factors including young athletic adults, head and neck surgery, obstructive sleep apnea, obesity, and airway pathology.", "Recognize the clinical and radiographic presentation of NPPE \u2014 tachypnea, hypoxemia, crackles, frothy or pink sputum, and diffuse bilateral infiltrates \u2014 and apply a structured differential diagnosis that includes aspiration, cardiogenic edema, fluid overload, residual neuromuscular blockade, bronchospasm, anaphylaxis, and pulmonary embolism.", "Apply an evidence-based treatment algorithm centered on relief of the airway obstruction, 100% oxygen, positive-pressure ventilation (CPAP / noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation for mild cases and tracheal intubation with PEEP for severe cases), and thoughtful use of adjuncts including furosemide and bronchodilators.", "Describe the pharmacology of the two most important rescue medications used when laryngospasm precipitates NPPE \u2014 propofol for deepening anesthesia and succinylcholine for breaking persistent laryngospasm \u2014 including doses, onset, and adverse effects (hyperkalemia, bradycardia, malignant hyperthermia trigger, prolonged paralysis with pseudocholinesterase deficiency, and pediatric considerations).", "Execute the seven-step laryngospasm treatment algorithm within the first minute of an obstructive event and prioritize the CRNA's role in smooth emergence, adequate reversal of neuromuscular blockade, and early recognition of the peri-extubation obstruction that most often precedes NPPE.", "Discuss post-treatment monitoring and disposition, including chest imaging, arterial blood gas indications, ICU escalation criteria, and typical resolution within 12 to 24 hours after obstruction relief and supportive care."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module7", "page_count": 25, "document_sha256": "36ee7a13cae6accc31fc44431590e8afa9baf872a445f2672db14d290af5d20e", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_2_module_8", "number": 8, "title": "Amniotic Fluid Embolism (AFE)", "short_title": "Amniotic Fluid Embolism", "credits": 2.5, "pharmacology_credits": 1.0, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Define amniotic fluid embolism (AFE) and describe its epidemiology, timing, incidence, mortality, and clinical significance for CRNA practice.", "Explain the pathophysiology of AFE, including pulmonary vascular obstruction, right ventricular failure, inflammatory activation, hemodynamic collapse, and disseminated intravascular coagulation.", "Distinguish the two clinical phases of AFE (Phase 1 cardiopulmonary collapse and Phase 2 hemostatic failure) and their implications for CRNA management priorities.", "Identify disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) associated with AFE and describe protocolized CRNA management including massive transfusion, fibrinogen replacement, and viscoelastic-guided therapy.", "Recognize reported risk-factor associations for AFE and understand why risk factors inform preparedness rather than reliable prediction.", "Apply diagnostic criteria for suspected AFE, including the Clark consensus criteria, while acknowledging that AFE is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion.", "Construct a rapid differential diagnosis for peripartum collapse, including hemorrhage, high spinal, LAST, anaphylaxis, PE, VAE, eclampsia, sepsis, aspiration, and cardiac disease.", "Perform immediate CRNA management of suspected AFE, including airway control, high-quality CPR with pregnancy modifications, manual uterine displacement, and activation of the obstetric emergency response.", "Implement advanced management of AFE including vasopressor and inotrope selection, right-ventricular support, inhaled pulmonary vasodilators, and consideration of ECMO for refractory shock.", "Coordinate perimortem cesarean delivery (resuscitative hysterotomy) and interprofessional obstetric emergency response.", "Discuss short-term and long-term maternal and neonatal outcomes after AFE, including morbidity, mortality, and psychological sequelae.", "Apply case-based reasoning to suspected AFE scenarios during labor, cesarean delivery, and the immediate postpartum period.", "Describe the AFE Registry and Biorepository and the CRNA's role in specimen preservation without interrupting resuscitation.", "Execute post-event responsibilities including ICU handoff, documentation, family communication, debriefing, and quality-improvement follow-through."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module8", "page_count": 34, "document_sha256": "5ef8e488a1855dcf8e6b4fbe7801c066e35a5065bd476b64d4205455906506b5", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_2_module_9", "number": 9, "title": "Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST)", "short_title": "Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity", "credits": 2.0, "pharmacology_credits": 1.0, "pain_credits": 1.0, "objectives": ["Describe the epidemiology and clinical significance of local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) across perioperative, obstetric, procedural, and non-operating room environments.", "Explain how local anesthetic pharmacology \u2014 including sodium channel blockade, lipid solubility, protein binding, pKa, and metabolism \u2014 contributes to central nervous system and cardiovascular toxicity.", "Differentiate amide from ester local anesthetics and describe how cumulative dosing, additive systemic toxicity, and physiologic state influence LAST susceptibility.", "Identify patient-, drug-, dose-, and procedure-related risk factors for LAST, including fascial plane blocks, continuous catheters, IV lidocaine infusions, and liposomal bupivacaine.", "Differentiate early neurologic findings from cardiovascular manifestations and atypical or masked presentations of LAST.", "Apply the ASRA 2020 LAST checklist principles for immediate CRNA management: airway support, seizure suppression with benzodiazepines, modified advanced cardiac life support, and early 20% lipid emulsion.", "Calculate 20% lipid emulsion bolus and infusion dosing for adults under 70 kg and 70 kg or greater, including escalation, cumulative dose limits, and post-stabilization monitoring.", "Modify advanced cardiac life support for LAST: use small-dose epinephrine, prefer amiodarone over lidocaine, and avoid vasopressin, beta-blockers, and calcium channel blockers.", "Use recommended maximum local anesthetic dose limits as an upper boundary \u2014 not a target \u2014 and reduce doses for high-risk patients and high-vascularity injection sites.", "Construct a rapid differential diagnosis for peri-injection collapse including high spinal, intravascular epinephrine, anaphylaxis, methemoglobinemia, seizure, cardiac event, and medication error.", "Develop prevention plans for regional anesthesia, fascial plane blocks, infiltration anesthesia, topical airway anesthesia, and continuous local anesthetic infusions, including cumulative dose tracking and handoff communication.", "Coordinate the LAST response as a team leader: role assignment, cognitive aid use, communication with surgical/procedural teams, and escalation to ECMO or expert consultation when needed.", "Apply case-based reasoning to LAST scenarios including early CNS toxicity from peripheral nerve blocks, cardiovascular collapse under sedation, and delayed LAST from IV lidocaine infusions.", "Execute post-event responsibilities including monitored observation, documentation, structured handoff, family communication, debriefing, and quality-improvement follow-through."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module9", "page_count": 36, "document_sha256": "afe155d5b1c7f0cf9058f8200b710c63e176f92241eeafdb972e7db048497a33", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_2_module_10", "number": 10, "title": "Malignant Hyperthermia (MH)", "short_title": "Malignant Hyperthermia", "credits": 3.0, "pharmacology_credits": 1.5, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Describe the genetic and pathophysiologic basis of malignant hyperthermia (MH), including RYR1, CACNA1S, and STAC3 variants and sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium dysregulation.", "Identify all recognized MH triggering anesthetic agents and list non-triggering (MH-safe) agents and techniques appropriate for MH-susceptible patients.", "Recognize the earliest clinical and monitored signs of an intraoperative MH crisis, including unexplained rise in end-tidal carbon dioxide, sinus tachycardia, masseter spasm, and mixed acidosis.", "Formulate a stepwise Malignant Hyperthermia Association of the United States (MHAUS) emergency response, including hotline activation, discontinuation of triggers, hyperventilation, and dantrolene administration.", "Calculate weight-based dantrolene dosing and compare reconstitution and administration of traditional dantrolene 20 mg vials with Ryanodex 250 mg vials.", "Implement adjunctive pharmacologic and physiologic management for MH, including treatment of hyperkalemia, acidosis, dysrhythmias, and hyperthermia, while avoiding calcium channel blockers.", "Evaluate the differential diagnosis for intraoperative hypermetabolism and distinguish MH from thyroid storm, sepsis, pheochromocytoma, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome.", "Coordinate post-crisis care, including ICU-level monitoring, continued dantrolene therapy, surveillance for recrudescence, and referral of the patient and biologic family for MH testing at an MHAUS-recognized center.", "Develop a comprehensive anesthetic plan for an MH-susceptible patient, including preoperative assessment, anesthesia machine preparation, non-triggering total intravenous anesthesia or regional technique, and PACU disposition.", "Apply case-based reasoning to classic intraoperative MH, ambulatory surgery center MH, and delayed postoperative MH with recrudescence, and confirm minimum dantrolene stocking at every anesthetizing location."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 31, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course2_module10", "page_count": 35, "document_sha256": "983bc7dbc3c0517be41413f49509a0ddd6e4b38a4cb21fb73747e830add51952", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}], "course_id": "1047241", "course_number": 2, "total_credits": 20, "pharmacology_credits": 10.5, "pain_credits": 1, "required_modules": 10, "purchases_enabled": false, "released": false}''');
final Map<String,dynamic> demoCourse2Banks = jsonDecode(r'''{"course_2_module_1": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which multi-society document is the current U.S. reference standard for brain death / death by neurologic criteria (BD/DNC) determination in both pediatric and adult patients? ]", "choice_a": "2023 AAN/AAP/CNS/SCCM multi-society consensus practice guideline", "choice_b": "1995 AAN adult brain death parameters (unrevised)", "choice_c": "2010 AAN adult and 2011 AAP/CNS/SCCM pediatric guidelines used separately", "choice_d": "2020 ASA statement on controlled DCD", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Greer et al. (2023) unified pediatric and adult BD/DNC determination for the first time in a single multi-society consensus practice guideline, updating the 2010 adult AAN parameters and 2011 pediatric guidelines.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "According to the 2023 NEJM randomized trial by Dhar and colleagues in 838 hemodynamically unstable brain-dead heart donors, IV levothyroxine compared with saline placebo: ]", "choice_a": "Was associated with a large reduction in vasopressor requirement", "choice_b": "Did NOT significantly increase the number of hearts transplanted and was associated with more severe hypertension and tachycardia", "choice_c": "Significantly increased the number of hearts transplanted", "choice_d": "Significantly improved donor ejection fraction", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Hearts were transplanted from 54.9% vs 53.2% of donors (adjusted RR 1.01, 95% CI 0.97\u20131.07, p=0.57). Severe hypertension and tachycardia were more common with levothyroxine. Routine IV levothyroxine is no longer supported for unstable brain-dead heart donors.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "A brain-dead donor develops abrupt polyuria of 500 mL/h with a rising serum sodium of 158 mEq/L. Which drip is most appropriate for the underlying pathophysiology? ]", "choice_a": "Nitroglycerin", "choice_b": "Dobutamine", "choice_c": "Vasopressin", "choice_d": "Norepinephrine", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "This is diabetes insipidus from posterior pituitary failure. Vasopressin replaces ADH, decreases free-water losses, and simultaneously supports blood pressure in vasodilatory shock.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A first-line vasoactive infusion for persistent hypotension in the brain-dead donor is most commonly: ]", "choice_a": "Milrinone", "choice_b": "Phenylephrine", "choice_c": "Isoproterenol", "choice_d": "Norepinephrine", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Norepinephrine is the most common first-line vasopressor for persistent hypotension in donor management protocols.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which anesthetic principle is TRUE for the brain-dead donor in the operating room? ]", "choice_a": "Medications are directed toward physiologic stability and organ preservation rather than treatment of pain or awareness", "choice_b": "Neuromuscular blockade is contraindicated", "choice_c": "Volatile anesthetics are mandatory throughout the case", "choice_d": "Opioids and sedative-hypnotics are required to prevent awareness", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "After BD/DNC declaration the donor is dead; medications are used to stabilize physiology and preserve organs. Spinal reflex movements may occur, so NMB is often used, but opioids/sedatives are not required for awareness or analgesia.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "In the 2025 U.S. nationwide analysis of 21,010 DCD kidney transplants by Kusakabe and colleagues, NRP was associated with which change in delayed graft function compared with non-NRP DCD kidneys? ]", "choice_a": "DGF could not be assessed because of small sample size", "choice_b": "DGF decreased from 49.7% to 30.3%", "choice_c": "DGF increased from 30.3% to 49.7%", "choice_d": "DGF was unchanged", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Kusakabe et al. (2026) reported DGF of 30.3% with NRP vs 49.7% without NRP, with also improved 3-year graft and patient survival.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which sequence of organ removal is most typical during full multi-organ procurement? ]", "choice_a": "Liver \u2192 thoracic organs \u2192 kidneys \u2192 pancreas \u2192 vessels/tissues", "choice_b": "Pancreas \u2192 kidneys \u2192 thoracic organs \u2192 liver \u2192 tissues", "choice_c": "Thoracic organs \u2192 liver/intestine \u2192 pancreas \u2192 kidneys \u2192 vessels/tissues", "choice_d": "Kidneys \u2192 liver \u2192 thoracic organs \u2192 pancreas \u2192 tissues", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The typical sequence reflects relative ischemic tolerance: thoracic organs first (most time-sensitive), then liver/intestine, then pancreas, then kidneys, then vessels and tissues.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "A central procedural requirement of TA-NRP that distinguishes it ethically from cardiopulmonary bypass in a living patient is: ]", "choice_a": "Discontinuation of all monitoring before flow is initiated", "choice_b": "Use of a heparin-free circuit", "choice_c": "Avoidance of vasoactive medications during perfusion", "choice_d": "Prevention of any return of cerebral blood flow", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Because circulation is restored after circulatory death, programs must surgically exclude cerebral circulation (e.g., aortic arch-vessel ligation and venting) before initiating perfusion to comply with the dead donor rule.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which agent supports blood pressure AND directly treats diabetes insipidus in the brain-dead donor? ]", "choice_a": "Vasopressin", "choice_b": "Norepinephrine", "choice_c": "Dopamine", "choice_d": "Phenylephrine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Vasopressin provides both vasoconstriction (V1) and antidiuretic activity (V2), making it uniquely useful in donors with vasoplegia plus diabetes insipidus.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "In the DCD pathway, current U.S. practice commonly requires a no-touch observation period AFTER circulatory arrest before death is declared. That observation period is typically: ]", "choice_a": "2 minutes", "choice_b": "5 minutes", "choice_c": "20 minutes", "choice_d": "30 seconds", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A 5-minute no-touch observation period is the common U.S. standard to minimize the risk of autoresuscitation while preserving public trust and procedural consistency.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which organ has the LONGEST typical out-of-body preservation window in DCD workflow? ]", "choice_a": "Heart", "choice_b": "Liver", "choice_c": "Kidneys", "choice_d": "Lungs", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Kidneys tolerate the longest cold ischemia (~24\u201336 h), which is why they are usually recovered last in the multi-organ sequence.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "During TA-NRP, which team member is most directly responsible for managing extracorporeal circuit flow, sweep, and hemofilter parameters? ]", "choice_a": "OPO coordinator", "choice_b": "Thoracic surgeon", "choice_c": "CRNA", "choice_d": "Perfusionist", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The perfusionist manages the extracorporeal circuit; the CRNA supports metabolic, hemodynamic, and documentation goals in parallel and communicates with the perfusion team.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Excessive crystalloid administration should specifically be AVOIDED when which organ is being considered for procurement? ]", "choice_a": "Lungs", "choice_b": "Pancreas", "choice_c": "Liver", "choice_d": "Kidneys", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Fluid overload worsens pulmonary edema and reduces lung suitability. Balanced resuscitation is preferred; excess crystalloid is avoided when lungs are planned for recovery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "In the 2024 UNOS analysis of 918 DCD heart transplants by Madan and colleagues, 1-year survival with NRP versus direct procurement and perfusion (DPP) was approximately: ]", "choice_a": "NRP 85% vs DPP 85%", "choice_b": "NRP 95.2% vs DPP 90.1%", "choice_c": "NRP 100% vs DPP 100%", "choice_d": "NRP 78% vs DPP 85%", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "NRP 1-year survival was 95.2% (95% CI 92.3\u201398.2) vs DPP 90.1% (87.4\u201392.8), log-rank p=0.005; adjusted HR 0.39 (95% CI 0.22\u20130.70), p=0.002. Donor heart discard was 1.4% NRP vs 9.6% DPP.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A CRNA is asked to administer heparin for a TA-NRP case. The correct action is to: ]", "choice_a": "Administer heparin immediately at the start of the withdrawal phase", "choice_b": "Give heparin only after cross-clamp", "choice_c": "Administer heparin per program protocol \u2014 typically after death declaration and prior to cannulation", "choice_d": "Refuse to administer heparin because it violates the dead donor rule", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Heparin timing in TA-NRP is program-specific but is typically given after death declaration and prior to cannulation. Giving heparin before death declaration for donation purposes may violate ethical safeguards; program protocol takes precedence.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which finding most likely indicates diabetes insipidus in a brain-dead donor? ]", "choice_a": "Polyuria with severe hyponatremia and concentrated urine", "choice_b": "Anuria with rising serum sodium", "choice_c": "Bradycardia with hyperkalemia", "choice_d": "Abrupt polyuria (>4 mL/kg/h) with rising serum sodium and dilute urine", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Central diabetes insipidus after brain death causes abrupt polyuria with hypernatremia and dilute urine due to loss of ADH secretion.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which statement about MAP targets in donor management is most accurate based on current consensus statements? ]", "choice_a": "Consensus statements differ: SCCM/ACCP/AOPO 2015 and JHLT 2020 use >60 mmHg while the Canadian 2020 guideline uses >65 mmHg", "choice_b": "MAP >50 mmHg is universally accepted", "choice_c": "MAP targets are not recommended in any consensus statement", "choice_d": "All consensus statements agree on MAP >70 mmHg", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "No universally accepted donor management goal bundle exists; MAP targets vary by consensus statement (SCCM/ACCP/AOPO 2015 >60, JHLT 2020 >60, Canadian 2020 >65).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "In the Brazilian cluster-randomized DONORS trial of 1,535 potential brain-dead donors, the subgroup with checklist adherence >79% had a cardiac-arrest rate of: ]", "choice_a": "20% versus 25%", "choice_b": "5.3% versus 14.8% in comparison groups (p=0.006)", "choice_c": "Not reported", "choice_d": "No difference between groups", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "High checklist adherence was associated with a cardiac-arrest rate of 5.3% vs 14.8% (p=0.006) and higher organ yield, even though the overall trial did not significantly reduce the primary endpoint.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which DCD NRP subtype is used specifically to recover BOTH thoracic and abdominal organs including the heart? ]", "choice_a": "Extracorporeal support for cerebral protection", "choice_b": "Cerebral NRP", "choice_c": "Thoracoabdominal NRP (TA-NRP)", "choice_d": "Abdominal NRP (A-NRP)", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "TA-NRP restores circulation to thoracic and abdominal organs so the heart and lungs, in addition to abdominal organs, can be evaluated and recovered in situ. A-NRP excludes the thoracic circulation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which statement about the ethical framework for TA-NRP is TRUE? ]", "choice_a": "The clinician declaring death may also be part of the transplant team", "choice_b": "Consent is not required for NRP because the donor is already dead", "choice_c": "The dead donor rule does not apply to NRP", "choice_d": "The decision to withdraw life-sustaining treatment must be made independently of the decision to donate", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Foundational ethical safeguards include (a) the decision to withdraw life-sustaining treatment must be made independently of the decision to donate, (b) the clinician declaring death must be separate from the transplant team, and (c) the dead donor rule remains absolute.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which medication is commonly continued as scheduled dosing or infusion in donor-management protocols to reduce inflammatory activation, improve vascular responsiveness, and \u2014 in some programs \u2014 support lung recovery? ]", "choice_a": "Methylprednisolone", "choice_b": "Furosemide", "choice_c": "Fentanyl", "choice_d": "Rocuronium", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Corticosteroids such as methylprednisolone are commonly used in donor-management protocols for their anti-inflammatory and vascular-responsiveness effects and, in some pathways, to support lung recovery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which term describes the period when organ perfusion becomes critically inadequate (severe hypotension, hypoxemia, or both) until cold flush or reperfusion begins in DCD? ]", "choice_a": "Agonal phase", "choice_b": "Functional warm ischemia", "choice_c": "Cold ischemia", "choice_d": "Asystolic time", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Functional warm ischemia is defined by physiologic thresholds and is often more clinically relevant than the total agonal interval because organ injury depends on how long perfusion is insufficient.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT an appropriate CRNA action before cross-clamp in a DBD procurement case? ]", "choice_a": "Anticipating hypotension during positioning, incision, and sternotomy", "choice_b": "Confirming vasoactive infusions and backup medications are available", "choice_c": "Administering opioid boluses because the donor may 'feel pain' from surgical stimulation", "choice_d": "Communicating with abdominal and thoracic teams before cannulation and cross-clamp", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Opioid boluses are not required because the BD/DNC donor cannot experience pain or awareness. Spinal reflex movements may occur; neuromuscular blockade and vasoactive management are the appropriate responses.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which statement about NRP versus normothermic machine perfusion (NMP) of DCD kidneys is supported by the 2025 Transplantation systematic review (Rijkse et al.)? ]", "choice_a": "Both had DGF rates >80%", "choice_b": "Neither modality improved on cold static storage", "choice_c": "NMP DGF was lower than NRP DGF", "choice_d": "NRP DGF (~24.6%) was lower than NMP DGF (~54.3%) with similar graft utilization", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The systematic review reported NRP DGF ~24.6% vs NMP DGF ~54.3%, with similar primary nonfunction and graft utilization; both are characterized as feasible strategies likely improved compared with cold-stored DCD kidneys.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_1", "question_id": "course_2_module_1_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which action best exemplifies protocolized, evidence-informed CRNA practice during donor procurement based on contemporary (2023\u20132026) evidence? ]", "choice_a": "Following a program-specific donor-management checklist and documenting time-critical events in real time", "choice_b": "Deferring lung-protective ventilation once BD/DNC is declared", "choice_c": "Empirically starting IV levothyroxine on every unstable brain-dead heart donor", "choice_d": "Avoiding vasopressors to prevent 'over-treating' the donor", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Contemporary evidence (DONORS trial, Lele et al. 2025 multicenter analysis) supports protocolized, checklist-driven donor care with meticulous real-time documentation. Routine IV levothyroxine is no longer supported (Dhar et al. 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_2": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which category of blood products is universally refused by Jehovah's Witnesses regardless of source (allogeneic, directed, or predeposited autologous)? ]", "choice_a": "Whole blood, red cells, plasma, platelets, and white cells", "choice_b": "Fibrinogen concentrate and PCC", "choice_c": "Recombinant clotting factors", "choice_d": "Albumin and immunoglobulins", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The primary blood components \u2014 whole blood, RBCs, plasma, platelets, and WBCs \u2014 are universally refused by JW patients regardless of source. Minor fractions and recombinant products are matters of individual conscience.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A JW patient states she 'refuses blood.' Which is the correct next step? ]", "choice_a": "Document the refusal in the chart and proceed with routine care", "choice_b": "Conduct a structured product-by-product review of primary components, minor fractions, autologous techniques, and hemostatic pharmacology", "choice_c": "Order predeposited autologous units to avoid transfusion", "choice_d": "Accept the statement as covering all blood-related products", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A blanket 'refuses blood' statement does not answer individual questions about minor fractions (albumin, fibrinogen concentrate, PCC, cryoprecipitate), autologous techniques (ANH, cell salvage, CPB), or nonhuman hemostatic products. Product-by-product consent is standard.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Per the 2024 EACTS/EACTAIC PBM guideline for cardiac surgery, which two interventions are most commonly accepted by JW patients? ]", "choice_a": "Whole blood and directed donor RBCs", "choice_b": "Predeposited autologous units and platelets", "choice_c": "Coagulation factor concentrates and intraoperative cell salvage", "choice_d": "Fresh frozen plasma and cryoprecipitate", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Casselman et al. (2025) state that most JW patients accept coagulation factor concentrates and intraoperative cell salvage \u2014 the two techniques central to modern bloodless cardiac surgery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "In the Gemelli et al. (2024) meta-analysis of 780 JW and 1,182 non-JW cardiac surgery patients, what was the perioperative mortality difference? ]", "choice_a": "JW patients had significantly lower mortality (OR 0.42)", "choice_b": "Mortality was not reported", "choice_c": "JW patients had significantly higher mortality (OR 2.14)", "choice_d": "No significant difference (OR 0.91, 95% CI 0.55\u20131.52, p=0.72)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The 2024 meta-analysis found no significant mortality difference (OR 0.91, p=0.72), demonstrating that protocolized PBM in JW patients achieves outcomes comparable to transfusion-accepting controls.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "A JW patient with mild preoperative anemia (Hb 10.2 g/dL) is scheduled for surgery in 3 weeks. Which intervention has the highest yield for correcting anemia in this window? ]", "choice_a": "IV ferric carboxymaltose 1,000 mg, repeated at 1 week if needed", "choice_b": "Fresh frozen plasma transfusion", "choice_c": "Oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg daily", "choice_d": "Delay surgery for 12 weeks and use oral iron alone", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "IV iron (e.g., ferric carboxymaltose) raises Hb rapidly. In Palaia et al. (2022), JW patients receiving FCM had a mean Hb rise of 2.3 g/dL over a mean 20 days. Oral iron requires 6\u20138 weeks and hepcidin blockade limits daily dosing (Evans & Mu\u00f1oz, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which oral iron regimen is preferred to maximize absorption and reduce hepcidin-mediated blockade? ]", "choice_a": "325 mg ferrous sulfate three times daily", "choice_b": "40\u201350 mg elemental iron daily OR 80\u2013100 mg alternate-day", "choice_c": "Combined oral iron and vitamin B12 twice daily", "choice_d": "IV push of 500 mg elemental iron once weekly", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Evans & Mu\u00f1oz (2025) describe hepcidin blockade lasting 24+ hours after a high oral iron dose. Alt-day dosing (80\u2013100 mg) or low-dose daily (40\u201350 mg) maximizes absorption and tolerance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "What is the typical FDA-approved epoetin alfa regimen for preoperative optimization in an elective non-cardiac surgical patient? ]", "choice_a": "100 IU/kg IV daily for 7 days", "choice_b": "10,000 IU IV once at induction", "choice_c": "600 IU/kg SC weekly \u00d7 3 doses ending 1 day before surgery, with concurrent iron", "choice_d": "40,000 IU SC single dose intraoperatively", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The FDA-approved regimen is epoetin alfa 600 IU/kg SC weekly \u00d7 3 doses ending 1 day preop, or 300 IU/kg daily \u00d7 10 days preop + 4 days postop, always with iron.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "In the ATACAS trial, high-dose tranexamic acid (100 mg/kg) compared with low-dose (50 mg/kg) in cardiac surgery produced which finding? ]", "choice_a": "No difference in transfusion or seizures", "choice_b": "Higher mortality with high-dose TXA", "choice_c": "Significantly fewer seizures with high-dose TXA", "choice_d": "Significantly reduced red-cell transfusion (21.6% vs 26.0%) with high-dose, but higher seizure rate (1.0% vs 0.4%, p=0.05)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Casselman et al. (2025) report that high-dose TXA reduced RBC transfusion but was associated with an increased seizure rate; high dose is not safer regarding seizures.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "What is the correct TXA regimen for trauma per Saxena et al. (2025) and CRASH-2? ]", "choice_a": "1 g IV within 3 hours of injury, then 1 g IV over 8 hours", "choice_b": "2 g IV bolus at hospital arrival", "choice_c": "50 mg/kg IV bolus regardless of setting", "choice_d": "10 mg/kg IV single dose regardless of time from injury", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "TXA 1 g IV within 3 h of injury followed by 1 g over 8 h is the standard trauma regimen. TXA administered >3 h after injury is not indicated and may be harmful.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Per Lawal et al. (2024), what is the doctrinal requirement that most JW patients apply to acute normovolemic hemodilution? ]", "choice_a": "Blood may be stored for up to 72 hours before reinfusion", "choice_b": "The blood circuit must remain continuous with the patient's circulation throughout collection and reinfusion", "choice_c": "ANH is universally refused", "choice_d": "Only crystalloid replacement is allowed with no blood withdrawal", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Lawal et al. (2024) explicitly state that the blood circuit must remain continuous for ANH to be acceptable to many JW adherents. Their protocol maintained a slow drip (4 drops/min) during the donation phase.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A JW patient consents to cell salvage. Which practice best preserves the continuous-circuit doctrinal requirement? ]", "choice_a": "Disconnect the reinfusion bag from IV tubing between collections", "choice_b": "Store salvaged blood in the OR refrigerator until end of case", "choice_c": "Keep the tubing from suction through wash bowl through reinfusion physically connected as one continuous circuit throughout the case", "choice_d": "Return salvaged blood only after the patient has emerged from anesthesia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "A continuously connected cell-salvage circuit is the operational implementation of the JW doctrinal requirement. Disconnection or storage typically renders the salvaged blood unacceptable.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which product is NONHUMAN in origin and universally acceptable to JW patients per the 2025 ASH Hematology Education Program? ]", "choice_a": "Prothrombin complex concentrate", "choice_b": "Fibrinogen concentrate", "choice_c": "Cryoprecipitate", "choice_d": "Recombinant factor VIIa (NovoSeven)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Recombinant factor VIIa is a nonhuman recombinant product. Fibrinogen concentrate, PCC, and cryoprecipitate are human-plasma-derived and are matters of individual conscience.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which product is a HUMAN-plasma-derived fraction whose acceptability must be individually confirmed with each JW patient? ]", "choice_a": "Fibrinogen concentrate", "choice_b": "Tranexamic acid", "choice_c": "Desmopressin", "choice_d": "Recombinant factor VIII", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Fibrinogen concentrate is human-plasma-derived. It is not universally accepted or refused by JW patients \u2014 it must be reviewed individually.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "In a JW patient with catastrophic hemorrhage refractory to norepinephrine and vasopressin, Saxena et al. (2025) describe which last-line rescue for refractory vasoplegia? ]", "choice_a": "Emergency transfusion of packed red blood cells", "choice_b": "Methylene blue", "choice_c": "Intravenous calcium chloride", "choice_d": "Sodium bicarbonate 8.4%", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Saxena et al. (2025) describe methylene blue as a last-line rescue for refractory vasoplegia when norepinephrine and vasopressin have failed in the JW trauma patient with acute blood-loss anemia.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A competent 45-year-old JW adult presents for elective surgery and refuses all blood products. What is the correct role of the anesthesia team? ]", "choice_a": "Proceed only after a court order overrides the refusal", "choice_b": "Administer blood if catastrophic hemorrhage develops intraoperatively", "choice_c": "Honor the documented refusal, provide all non-refused interventions, and document the plan clearly", "choice_d": "Refuse to proceed with the surgery", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "A competent adult has the right to refuse life-saving transfusion. The anesthesia team must honor the documented refusal even in the setting of catastrophic hemorrhage, while providing all interventions the patient has accepted.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which U.S. Supreme Court case established the constitutional interest in refusing unwanted medical treatment that underlies an adult's right to refuse blood transfusion? ]", "choice_a": "Prince v. Massachusetts (1944)", "choice_b": "Roe v. Wade (1973)", "choice_c": "Griswold v. Connecticut (1965)", "choice_d": "Cruzan v. Director, Missouri Department of Health (1990)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Cruzan v. Director, Missouri Department of Health (1990) recognized a constitutional liberty interest in refusing unwanted medical treatment. Prince v. Massachusetts (1944) established that parents cannot refuse life-saving treatment for their children.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "A 4-year-old JW child requires life-saving transfusion. Parents refuse. Which principle governs? ]", "choice_a": "Parents cannot make martyrs of their children (Prince v. Massachusetts); courts routinely override refusal in the child's best interest", "choice_b": "Only the treating physician's judgment matters", "choice_c": "The mature-minor doctrine allows the child to consent", "choice_d": "Parental refusal is absolute for pediatric patients", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Prince v. Massachusetts (1944) established that parents cannot make martyrs of their children. Courts routinely grant emergency orders authorizing transfusion in pediatric JW patients when refusal risks death or serious harm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "According to the Wheeler & Formstone (2025) review of English pediatric judicial responses, what recommendation is made regarding clinical judgment about compulsory transfusion? ]", "choice_a": "All pediatric JW patients should be transfused without court involvement", "choice_b": "Clinical judgment should be centralized in pediatric hematology/oncology centers; clinicians should avoid seeking rolling or enduring authority", "choice_c": "Court involvement is never appropriate in pediatric JW cases", "choice_d": "Clinicians should always seek a rolling authority to compel transfusion in JW children", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Wheeler & Formstone (2025) recommend centralization of clinical judgment in pediatric heme/onc centers and caution against seeking 'rolling or enduring authority' to compel transfusion; each case should be evaluated on its own facts.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which of the four PBM pillars specifically addresses 'patient-centered decision-making' in the JW context? ]", "choice_a": "Pillar 2 \u2014 minimize blood loss", "choice_b": "Pillar 1 \u2014 optimize hematopoiesis", "choice_c": "Pillar 4 \u2014 patient-centered decision-making (product-by-product consent, HLC involvement, advance directive)", "choice_d": "Pillar 3 \u2014 optimize physiologic tolerance of anemia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Pillar 4 focuses on the shared, patient-centered decisions that shape the entire PBM plan for the JW patient. All other pillars flow from what the patient has consented to.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A pregnant JW woman refuses blood transfusion for herself. What is the U.S. legal framework? ]", "choice_a": "The state may compel transfusion to protect the fetus", "choice_b": "The husband can override her refusal", "choice_c": "The obstetrician makes the decision unilaterally", "choice_d": "A competent adult mother retains the right to refuse transfusion for herself, even if refusal may harm the fetus", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Most U.S. courts have treated the pregnant JW woman as having full adult autonomy over her own body; her refusal of transfusion for herself must be honored even if it risks fetal harm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which of the following ANH parameters is appropriate for a 70 kg adult JW patient with Hb 12 g/dL undergoing surgery with expected blood loss of 1 L? ]", "choice_a": "Withdraw approximately 500\u20131,000 mL of whole blood with isovolemic crystalloid replacement", "choice_b": "Withdraw 2,000 mL of whole blood before induction", "choice_c": "Withdraw 100 mL and discard", "choice_d": "Withdraw 1 unit and store in the OR refrigerator for 24 hours", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Standard ANH withdraws approximately 10\u201315 mL/kg (500\u20131000 mL for a 70 kg adult) with continuous isovolemic crystalloid replacement. The withdrawn blood remains connected to the patient and is reinfused later in the case.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which is the WHO definition of severe anemia used to identify the point at which severe morbidity risk rises sharply? ]", "choice_a": "Hb <9 g/dL", "choice_b": "Hb <7 g/dL", "choice_c": "Hb <10 g/dL", "choice_d": "Hb <11 g/dL", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Per WHO and Palaia et al. (2022), severe anemia is defined as Hb <7 g/dL. This is the threshold at which JW patients in the Palaia protocol also received EPO in addition to IV iron.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of these is NOT an appropriate intraoperative blood-conservation strategy for a JW patient? ]", "choice_a": "Meticulous surgical hemostasis and topical hemostatics", "choice_b": "Point-of-care coagulation monitoring (TEG/ROTEM) to guide specific hemostatic use", "choice_c": "Empiric administration of fresh frozen plasma when hemoglobin drops", "choice_d": "Normothermia maintenance and avoidance of dilutional coagulopathy", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Fresh frozen plasma is a primary blood component and is universally refused by JW patients. Empiric FFP is inappropriate; hemostatic therapy should be POC-guided and drawn from JW-accepted products.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A JW patient in the ICU with severe anemia (Hb 5.4 g/dL) is hemodynamically stable. Which strategy best supports oxygen delivery? ]", "choice_a": "Administer packed red cells despite the refusal", "choice_b": "Withhold all oxygen therapy", "choice_c": "Discontinue vasopressors to reduce metabolic demand", "choice_d": "Increase FiO2 toward 1.0, optimize cardiac output, reduce oxygen consumption (sedation/paralysis), continue IV iron and EPO", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Oxygen-delivery optimization for the anemic JW patient combines maximum SpO2/FiO2, cardiac-output support with volume and vasoactives, reduction of oxygen consumption (sedation, paralysis, cooling in some cases), and continued erythropoiesis-stimulating therapy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_2", "question_id": "course_2_module_2_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which action best reflects protocolized, evidence-informed JW anesthesia practice consistent with the Gemelli et al. (2024) meta-analysis findings? ]", "choice_a": "Apply a structured multidisciplinary PBM protocol from preoperative optimization through postoperative recovery, documented and reconfirmed with the patient", "choice_b": "Skip preoperative iron optimization for time-sensitive cases", "choice_c": "Administer blood if a colleague believes it is medically necessary", "choice_d": "Cancel elective JW cases as too high-risk", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The favorable outcomes in Gemelli et al. (2024) resulted from strict, protocolized, multidisciplinary PBM applied at every stage \u2014 precisely the practice standard recommended in Casselman et al. (2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_3": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which biochemical test is recommended by the Endocrine Society as first-line screening for pheochromocytoma in a high-risk patient (e.g., known hereditary syndrome or prior PPGL)? ]", "choice_a": "Plasma free (fractionated) metanephrines", "choice_b": "Serum chromogranin A alone", "choice_c": "Serum catecholamines drawn during a symptom-free period", "choice_d": "24-hour urinary vanillylmandelic acid (VMA)", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Plasma free (fractionated) metanephrines have sensitivity 96\u201399% and are the recommended first-line screening test in high-risk patients per the Endocrine Society guideline (Lenders et al., 2014). 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines are the alternative first-line test in lower-risk screening. VMA and chromogranin A alone are not first-line.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which of the following is the correct sequence for preoperative pharmacologic optimization of a patient with pheochromocytoma? ]", "choice_a": "Simultaneous alpha and beta blockade with immediate surgery", "choice_b": "Alpha blockade first, then volume loading, then beta blockade if needed", "choice_c": "Metyrosine alone, no alpha or beta blockade", "choice_d": "Beta blockade first, then alpha blockade, then volume loading", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Alpha blockade must precede beta blockade. Starting beta blockade before alpha blockade removes vasodilating beta-2 activity while alpha-mediated vasoconstriction persists, producing catastrophic unopposed vasoconstriction. Volume loading follows alpha blockade to correct chronic hypovolemia.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which finding from the PRESCRIPT randomized trial (Buitenwerf et al., 2020) best summarizes the phenoxybenzamine-versus-doxazosin comparison? ]", "choice_a": "Doxazosin produced significantly lower perioperative mortality than phenoxybenzamine", "choice_b": "The two drugs produced identical hemodynamic profiles", "choice_c": "Phenoxybenzamine produced fewer intraoperative hypertensive episodes; doxazosin produced fewer postoperative hypotensive episodes; no difference in mortality or major cardiovascular events", "choice_d": "Phenoxybenzamine produced significantly lower perioperative mortality than doxazosin", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The PRESCRIPT trial (Buitenwerf 2020) found phenoxybenzamine yielded fewer intraoperative hypertensive episodes (a consequence of noncompetitive long-acting blockade) but more postoperative hypotension; doxazosin produced fewer postoperative hypotensive episodes. Mortality, MI, stroke, and ICU LOS were not different. Both agents are acceptable.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A patient with pheochromocytoma has completed 12 days of phenoxybenzamine 40 mg BID. Which set of findings best meets the Roizen criteria for preoperative readiness? ]", "choice_a": "BP 178/98 seated; new ST-T changes on ECG yesterday; 12 PVCs/min", "choice_b": "BP <120/80 with severe orthostasis to 70/40 on standing", "choice_c": "BP <165/90 but bradycardia to 42 bpm at rest", "choice_d": "BP <165/90 seated for 24\u201348 h; mild orthostasis; ECG stable for \u22651 week; <5 PVCs/min", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Roizen criteria: BP <165/90 seated for 24\u201348 hours, mild orthostasis only, no ST-T changes for \u22651 week, and <5 PVCs/min. Severe orthostasis or bradycardia indicates overshoot; residual hypertension with ECG changes indicates inadequate preparation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which drug is contraindicated (or should be avoided) in a patient with an inadequately alpha-blocked pheochromocytoma? ]", "choice_a": "Isolated beta blockade (e.g., metoprolol without alpha blockade)", "choice_b": "Sodium nitroprusside", "choice_c": "Clevidipine", "choice_d": "Nicardipine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Isolated beta blockade removes vasodilating beta-2 activity while alpha-mediated vasoconstriction is unopposed, causing hypertensive crisis, LV failure, and pulmonary edema. Nicardipine, clevidipine, and sodium nitroprusside are all appropriate for controlling hypertension in the pheochromocytoma patient.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "During adrenal vein ligation, which drug should the CRNA have drawn, labeled, and immediately available even before evidence of hypotension? ]", "choice_a": "Metoclopramide", "choice_b": "Vasopressin", "choice_c": "Additional volatile anesthetic", "choice_d": "Large-dose morphine bolus", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Vasopressin is particularly effective in the catecholamine-resistant vasoplegia that can follow adrenal vein ligation. It should always be prepared and labeled before this transition. Morphine (histamine release) and metoclopramide (dopamine antagonism) are problematic in pheochromocytoma.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which intraoperative event most often triggers a severe catecholamine surge during laparoscopic adrenalectomy for pheochromocytoma? ]", "choice_a": "Skin closure", "choice_b": "Antibiotic administration", "choice_c": "Tumor compression or mobilization and abdominal insufflation", "choice_d": "Emergence from anesthesia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The most dramatic hypertensive episodes occur during intubation, abdominal insufflation, incision, and direct tumor compression or mobilization. Adrenal vein ligation is the pivot point for the opposite problem \u2014 sudden hypotension.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "A patient develops profound hypotension (MAP 45) 5 minutes after adrenal vein ligation. Which initial pharmacologic response is most appropriate? ]", "choice_a": "Continue vasodilator infusion at current rate", "choice_b": "Bolus phenoxybenzamine to deepen alpha blockade", "choice_c": "Metoprolol IV to slow reflex tachycardia", "choice_d": "Norepinephrine infusion titrated to MAP goal, with vasopressin and fluid available; consider phenylephrine boluses", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Post-resection hypotension is multifactorial (catecholamine withdrawal, residual alpha blockade, anesthetic vasodilation, chronic hypovolemia). First-line management is norepinephrine infusion titrated to MAP goal, with vasopressin available for catecholamine-resistant vasoplegia and judicious fluid resuscitation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "During an elective cholecystectomy, BP acutely rises to 260/150 with new pulmonary edema at insufflation in a patient with no known pheochromocytoma history. Which is NOT part of the CRNA immediate-action bundle? ]", "choice_a": "Immediately administer metoprolol 5 mg IV as isolated therapy", "choice_b": "Deepen anesthesia and ask the surgeon to pause", "choice_c": "Start a short-acting antihypertensive (nicardipine, clevidipine, or phentolamine)", "choice_d": "Consider aborting the elective operation once stable and arranging endocrine workup", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Isolated beta blockade in a patient with a suspected undiagnosed pheochromocytoma (never alpha-blocked) is dangerous \u2014 it can worsen vasoconstriction. Deepening anesthesia, short-acting vasodilators, magnesium adjunct, surgeon pause, and consideration of aborting the elective case are all appropriate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Approximately what proportion of pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma cases in modern series are found to carry an identifiable germline mutation on multi-gene panel testing? ]", "choice_a": "About 1%", "choice_b": "About 30\u201340%", "choice_c": "About 80%", "choice_d": "About 10%", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Contemporary series using multi-gene panel sequencing find identifiable germline mutations in 30\u201340% of PPGL patients \u2014 substantially higher than the older \"10% familial\" teaching. Every PPGL patient should be offered genetic evaluation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which hereditary PPGL syndrome carries the highest risk of metastatic disease and often prompts consideration of systemic therapy such as belzutifan? ]", "choice_a": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)", "choice_b": "Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL)", "choice_c": "SDHB paraganglioma syndrome", "choice_d": "MEN2A (RET mutation)", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "SDHB-mutated paragangliomas carry the highest metastatic risk (up to ~40%) and are the most likely to require long-term surveillance and systemic therapy for advanced disease. MEN2, VHL, and NF1 are associated with PPGL but with different clinical patterns and lower metastatic risk.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Why does chronic pheochromocytoma often produce intravascular volume contraction despite hypertension? ]", "choice_a": "Excess catecholamines directly deplete plasma volume via renal excretion of albumin", "choice_b": "Catecholamines cause primary aldosterone deficiency", "choice_c": "Chronic hyperglycemia causes osmotic diuresis and hypovolemia", "choice_d": "Sustained alpha-1 vasoconstriction elevates blood pressure, the kidney responds with pressure diuresis, and the circulating volume falls", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Sustained alpha-1 vasoconstriction elevates blood pressure; the kidney responds with pressure diuresis, reducing intravascular volume 10\u201320% below normal over months to years. This contracted volume becomes clinically apparent when alpha blockade is instituted or when catecholamines fall at adrenal vein ligation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which of the following BEST describes the role of magnesium sulfate in pheochromocytoma anesthesia? ]", "choice_a": "Adjunct that reduces catecholamine release, blunts arrhythmia, provides mild vasodilation, and potentiates neuromuscular blockade", "choice_b": "First-line vasopressor for post-resection hypotension", "choice_c": "Contraindicated in pheochromocytoma", "choice_d": "Diagnostic agent to confirm biochemical pheochromocytoma", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Magnesium sulfate is a valuable adjunct in pheochromocytoma anesthesia \u2014 it reduces catecholamine release from chromaffin cells and adrenergic nerves, blunts arrhythmia, provides mild vasodilation, and potentiates neuromuscular blockade (reduce NMBA dose and monitor TOF).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which drug should be prepared and labeled specifically because adrenal vein ligation may be followed by catecholamine-resistant vasoplegia unresponsive to conventional adrenergic vasopressors? ]", "choice_a": "Dopamine", "choice_b": "Vasopressin", "choice_c": "Milrinone", "choice_d": "Metoprolol", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Vasopressin is non-adrenergic and effective when adrenergic responsiveness is blunted after tumor resection. Metoprolol worsens hemodynamics; dopamine may provoke arrhythmia; milrinone would worsen vasodilation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Which finding in the immediate postoperative period after pheochromocytoma resection is common enough to warrant scheduled monitoring for at least 24 hours? ]", "choice_a": "Hyperkalemia", "choice_b": "Malignant hyperthermia", "choice_c": "Rebound hypoglycemia from catecholamine withdrawal and restored insulin secretion", "choice_d": "Immediate return of hypertension in every patient", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Catecholamine excess inhibits insulin secretion and raises glucose. After tumor resection, insulin rebounds and hypoglycemia can be significant. Scheduled glucose monitoring every 1\u20132 hours for at least 24 h is standard, with special attention in diabetic patients.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which set of induction and airway drugs is generally considered safe in an ADEQUATELY prepared pheochromocytoma patient? ]", "choice_a": "Ketamine (high dose), ephedrine, succinylcholine, morphine 50 mg bolus", "choice_b": "Metoclopramide induction followed by isolated beta blockade", "choice_c": "Cocaine topical + high-dose morphine", "choice_d": "Propofol, fentanyl, rocuronium, sevoflurane", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Propofol, fentanyl, rocuronium, and a volatile such as sevoflurane are all appropriate in an optimized patient. High-dose ketamine, ephedrine, succinylcholine (fasciculations may compress the tumor), and large morphine boluses (histamine) are best avoided or used cautiously. Metoclopramide has case reports of provoking hypertensive crisis.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which imaging modality is preferred for a suspected PPGL in pregnancy or when iodinated contrast should be avoided? ]", "choice_a": "MRI abdomen (T2-weighted)", "choice_b": "Barium enema", "choice_c": "Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen/pelvis", "choice_d": "Bone scan", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "MRI (typically T2-weighted) is preferred in pregnancy, pediatric patients, contrast allergy, and head-and-neck paragangliomas. Contrast-enhanced CT is first-line in most adults with confirmed biochemistry.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Metyrosine (\u03b1-methyl-para-tyrosine) works by: ]", "choice_a": "Activating vasopressin V1 receptors", "choice_b": "Inhibiting tyrosine hydroxylase, thereby reducing catecholamine synthesis", "choice_c": "Antagonizing dopamine at D2 receptors", "choice_d": "Blocking alpha-1 adrenergic receptors", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Metyrosine inhibits tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine synthesis. Its use signals a high-burden case (very large tumor, extensive metastatic disease, or refractory symptoms). Sedation and crystalluria are the main dose-limiting side effects.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A CRNA is caring for a patient on chronic belzutifan therapy for metastatic paraganglioma who now presents for elective cholecystectomy. Which perioperative consideration is most specific to belzutifan? ]", "choice_a": "Contraindication to all volatile anesthetics", "choice_b": "Need for prophylactic anticoagulation", "choice_c": "Anemia (up to 90% of patients on belzutifan) \u2014 assess hemoglobin, plan for possible transfusion, consider oxygen-delivery reserve", "choice_d": "Significant risk of malignant hyperthermia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Belzutifan (HIF-2\u03b1 inhibitor, U.S. FDA approval May 2025) causes anemia in up to 90% of patients (grade 3+ in ~20%). Preoperative hemoglobin assessment and transfusion planning are the key perioperative considerations. It does not cause MH, contraindicate volatile agents, or mandate anticoagulation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which sequence best reflects the phase-specific transitions the CRNA should anticipate during laparoscopic pheochromocytoma resection? ]", "choice_a": "Skin closure \u2192 emergence \u2192 PACU pain control", "choice_b": "Regional block \u2192 sedation \u2192 local infiltration", "choice_c": "Antibiotic administration \u2192 warming blanket \u2192 OG tube \u2192 foley", "choice_d": "Induction and intubation \u2192 insufflation \u2192 tumor manipulation \u2192 adrenal vein ligation \u2192 post-resection hypotension", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The intraoperative course of pheochromocytoma resection is best understood as a series of predictable transitions: induction/intubation \u2192 insufflation \u2192 tumor manipulation \u2192 adrenal vein ligation \u2192 post-resection hypotension. The CRNA prepares the appropriate drug before each transition.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which vasopressor is often selected as first-line INFUSION for post-resection hypotension in the pheochromocytoma patient? ]", "choice_a": "Norepinephrine (0.02\u20130.5 mcg/kg/min)", "choice_b": "Dopamine at 20 mcg/kg/min", "choice_c": "Isoproterenol", "choice_d": "Milrinone alone", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Norepinephrine is the usual first-line infusion for post-resection vasodilatory hypotension because it provides strong vasopressor support with some beta-1 activity for cardiac output. Phenylephrine boluses and vasopressin infusion are complementary options.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following adrenal masses REQUIRES biochemical exclusion of pheochromocytoma BEFORE any biopsy, surgical resection, or adrenal manipulation? ]", "choice_a": "Only masses in patients over 65 years old", "choice_b": "Every adrenal incidentaloma identified on cross-sectional imaging", "choice_c": "Only masses greater than 10 cm", "choice_d": "Only masses in patients with known cancer elsewhere", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Approximately 5\u201310% of adrenal incidentalomas are pheochromocytomas. Biopsy or surgical manipulation of an unsuspected pheochromocytoma has been described as producing life-threatening catecholamine crisis. Every adrenal incidentaloma requires biochemical exclusion before intervention.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Roizen criteria include which of the following? ]", "choice_a": "BP consistently >180/110 for 24 hours", "choice_b": "Complete absence of any orthostatic change", "choice_c": "BP <165/90 seated for 24\u201348 h, mild orthostasis only, no ST-T changes for \u22651 week, <5 PVCs/min", "choice_d": "Ability to run one mile without symptoms", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The Roizen criteria are pragmatic clinical endpoints of adequate preoperative preparation: BP <165/90 seated for 24\u201348 hours, mild orthostasis only, no ST-T changes for \u22651 week, and <5 PVCs/min. They are not hard cutoffs but guide the decision to proceed with elective surgery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A patient with a paraganglioma is on doxazosin. During tumor manipulation, BP rises to 235/125 despite an infusion of nicardipine. Which additional agent is a reasonable next choice for this DISCRETE catecholamine-driven spike? ]", "choice_a": "Additional oral doxazosin", "choice_b": "Ephedrine bolus", "choice_c": "Metoclopramide", "choice_d": "Phentolamine 2\u20135 mg IV bolus", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Phentolamine provides acute alpha-adrenergic blockade and is particularly useful for discrete catecholamine-driven spikes during tumor handling. Anticipate hypotension once the stimulation stops. Additional oral doxazosin acts too slowly; ephedrine and metoclopramide are contraindicated/inappropriate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_3", "question_id": "course_2_module_3_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "After pheochromocytoma resection, biochemical cure should be confirmed by: ]", "choice_a": "Plasma or urinary metanephrines rechecked 2\u20136 weeks postoperatively, then annual lifelong surveillance", "choice_b": "A negative CT scan at 24 hours", "choice_c": "A single normal in-hospital blood pressure", "choice_d": "Discharge without vasopressor requirement", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Biochemical cure is confirmed by plasma or urinary metanephrines 2\u20136 weeks after surgery. Because up to 15% of patients (higher for hereditary or SDHB disease) develop recurrence or metastatic disease, the Endocrine Society guideline recommends lifelong annual biochemical surveillance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_4": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "A 24-year-old woman is scheduled for elective septoplasty. She reports lifelong easy bruising, epistaxis 4\u20135 times per year requiring cautery on two occasions, and heavy menses. Her PT and aPTT are normal, platelet count is 245,000. Which is the most appropriate next step?", "choice_a": "Order a validated bleeding-assessment tool (ISTH-BAT), VWF antigen, VWF activity, and FVIII activity before proceeding.", "choice_b": "Give empiric DDAVP 0.3 mcg/kg IV at induction to cover possible undiagnosed VWD.", "choice_c": "Cancel the case pending genetic testing for VWD.", "choice_d": "Proceed to surgery as scheduled \u2014 normal PT/aPTT excludes a clinically significant bleeding disorder.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Normal PT and aPTT do not exclude VWD. Per the ASH 2021 diagnosis guideline, a patient with a positive bleeding history and low pretest probability should have a validated bleeding assessment (ISTH-BAT \u22656 for adult female is positive) plus VWF antigen, VWF activity, and FVIII activity. Empiric DDAVP is not appropriate without documented response and could be harmful in Type 2B. Genetic testing is not the first-line diagnostic step.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which VWD subtype is characterized by a gain-of-function VWF mutation that binds platelets excessively, causing loss of high-molecular-weight multimers and intermittent thrombocytopenia?", "choice_a": "Type 2A", "choice_b": "Type 2B", "choice_c": "Type 1", "choice_d": "Type 2N", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Type 2B is the gain-of-function VWF variant with increased platelet GPIb binding, which consumes both HMW multimers and platelets. This is the subtype in which empiric DDAVP is generally contraindicated because DDAVP-induced VWF release can worsen platelet aggregation and thrombocytopenia. Type 2A also has HMW multimer loss but by a different mechanism and without thrombocytopenia.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "A patient with known Type 3 VWD is scheduled for elective cholecystectomy. What is the most appropriate preoperative hemostatic strategy?", "choice_a": "Fresh frozen plasma 15 mL/kg preoperatively.", "choice_b": "Tranexamic acid 1 g IV alone.", "choice_c": "Plasma-derived VWF/FVIII concentrate 40\u201360 IU/kg IV pre-incision with scheduled redosing and factor monitoring.", "choice_d": "DDAVP 0.3 mcg/kg IV 30 minutes before incision.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Type 3 VWD has near-complete absence of VWF; DDAVP is not effective because there are no endogenous stores to release. Plasma-derived VWF/FVIII concentrate (Humate-P, wilate, or Alphanate \u2014 though Alphanate is not indicated for major surgery in Type 3) is the standard. Vonvendi paired with recombinant FVIII is an alternative. TXA and FFP alone are inadequate for Type 3 major surgery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Per the ASH 2021 guideline, what VWF activity and FVIII targets should be maintained after major surgery in a patient with VWD, and for how long?", "choice_a": "FVIII \u226550 IU/dL only, VWF not monitored postoperatively.", "choice_b": "VWF and FVIII \u2265100 IU/dL for 14 days.", "choice_c": "VWF \u226530 IU/dL for 24 hours.", "choice_d": "VWF and FVIII \u226550 IU/dL for at least 3 consecutive days.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The ASH 2021 guideline, based on the Brignardello-Petersen 2022 systematic review, recommends maintaining VWF activity and FVIII activity \u22650.50 IU/mL (50 IU/dL) for at least 3 consecutive days after major surgery. Many programs continue for 7\u201314 days depending on procedure and bleeding, but the evidence-based minimum is 3 days at 50 IU/dL.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "A 68-year-old woman with severe aortic stenosis (mean gradient 55 mmHg) has had three episodes of GI bleeding from small-bowel angiodysplasia in the past year. Preoperative workup for TAVR shows VWF antigen 90 IU/dL, VWF activity 40 IU/dL, activity/antigen ratio 0.44, HMW multimer loss on multimer analysis. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "choice_a": "Heyde syndrome \u2014 severe AS with acquired VWS from HMW multimer shearing", "choice_b": "Late-onset Type 1 VWD", "choice_c": "Chronic disseminated intravascular coagulation", "choice_d": "Type 2N VWD", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Heyde syndrome is the clinical triad of severe aortic stenosis, GI bleeding from small-bowel angiodysplasia, and acquired VWS from mechanical shearing of HMW multimers across the stenotic valve. The activity/antigen ratio <0.7 and HMW multimer loss are the diagnostic laboratory pattern. TAVR (or surgical AVR) reliably reverses the acquired VWS within 24\u201372 hours as valvular shear normalizes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "A DDAVP-responsive Type 1 VWD patient receives DDAVP 0.3 mcg/kg IV for a dental extraction. Which perioperative management principle is most important?", "choice_a": "Give supplemental hypotonic fluid to prevent hypernatremia.", "choice_b": "Fluid-restrict to approximately 1 L/day, avoid hypotonic fluids, and monitor serum sodium.", "choice_c": "Combine with therapeutic-dose enoxaparin to prevent VTE.", "choice_d": "Give a second identical DDAVP dose in 4 hours to prolong the effect.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "DDAVP is antidiuretic and causes free-water retention; the specific hazard is symptomatic hyponatremia, particularly in children, older adults, and those with cardiovascular disease. Fluid restriction to ~1 L/day, avoidance of hypotonic fluids, and sodium monitoring are the standard precautions. Tachyphylaxis limits the benefit of a repeat dose within 24 hours. Anticoagulation is not indicated.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which of the following VWF replacement products does NOT contain factor VIII?", "choice_a": "Humate-P", "choice_b": "Alphanate", "choice_c": "Vonvendi (vonicog alfa, recombinant VWF)", "choice_d": "wilate", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Vonvendi is a recombinant human VWF product and does not contain FVIII. In elective surgery it is typically given 12\u201324 h preoperatively to allow endogenous FVIII to rise once VWF becomes available to stabilize it. In urgent surgery with low baseline FVIII, Vonvendi must be paired with a recombinant FVIII product to achieve immediate correction of both. Humate-P, wilate, and Alphanate are all plasma-derived and contain both VWF and FVIII.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Per ASH 2021, what is the recommended oral tranexamic acid regimen for a patient with mild VWD undergoing dental extraction?", "choice_a": "5 g PO loading dose once, no maintenance.", "choice_b": "500 mg PO once at the time of extraction only.", "choice_c": "1 g PO daily for 3 days.", "choice_d": "25 mg/kg (typically 1,000\u20131,300 mg) PO three times daily for 5\u20137 days.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "ASH 2021 recommends oral TXA at 25 mg/kg (typically 1,000\u20131,300 mg) three times daily for 5\u20137 days for dental extractions and similar mucosal procedures in patients with VWD. This antifibrinolytic coverage substantially reduces delayed mucosal rebleeding as the primary clot begins to dissolve.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "A parturient with well-controlled Type 1 VWD (VWF activity 115 IU/dL, FVIII 128 IU/dL at admission; institutional target \u2265100 IU/dL for neuraxial) receives a labor epidural without complication. Four hours postpartum she requests removal. What is the most important consideration before removing the catheter?", "choice_a": "Verify current VWF activity meets institutional threshold (\u2265100 IU/dL) before removal.", "choice_b": "Remove the catheter immediately \u2014 postpartum removal timing does not depend on factor levels.", "choice_c": "Give an additional DDAVP dose immediately before removal.", "choice_d": "Wait 24 hours regardless of factor levels.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Factor levels must be at institutional threshold BOTH at the time of neuraxial placement AND at the time of catheter removal. Postpartum VWF and FVIII can fall rapidly, and catheter removal below target is a described mechanism of delayed epidural hematoma. Verifying the current level before removal is the appropriate standard.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which multimodal analgesic strategy is most appropriate in a Type 1 VWD patient after major abdominal surgery?", "choice_a": "Aspirin 650 mg PO every 4 hours plus acetaminophen.", "choice_b": "Scheduled acetaminophen 1 g PO q6h, IV lidocaine infusion 1 mg/kg/h, opioid PCA, avoid NSAIDs.", "choice_c": "Ibuprofen 800 mg PO every 8 hours as primary analgesic.", "choice_d": "Scheduled ketorolac 30 mg IV every 6 hours for 48 hours plus opioid PRN.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "NSAIDs (ketorolac, ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac) reversibly inhibit platelet COX-1 for 24\u201372 hours, and this effect is additive to the VWD defect; they should be avoided unless hematology specifically agrees. Aspirin is irreversibly platelet-inhibiting and also avoided. Acetaminophen, IV lidocaine infusion, opioid PCA, low-dose ketamine, and local anesthetic techniques are all appropriate opioid-sparing alternatives.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A 55-year-old man on chronic left ventricular assist device (HeartMate 3) support presents with recurrent epistaxis. Which finding is expected?", "choice_a": "Prolonged PT due to factor VII inhibition.", "choice_b": "Isolated thrombocytopenia with normal VWF and FVIII.", "choice_c": "Loss of HMW VWF multimers on multimer analysis, consistent with LVAD-induced acquired VWS.", "choice_d": "Elevated VWF activity from LVAD-induced endothelial activation.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Continuous-flow LVADs (HeartMate II, HeartMate 3) produce high shear stress that mechanically cleaves the largest VWF multimers, causing acquired VWS with selective HMW multimer loss. This is a well-described contributor to mucosal and GI bleeding in LVAD patients. The activity/antigen ratio is characteristically reduced.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which pattern of laboratory findings is most consistent with Type 2N VWD?", "choice_a": "VWF antigen <10 IU/dL, VWF activity <10 IU/dL, FVIII 6 IU/dL.", "choice_b": "VWF antigen 30 IU/dL, VWF activity 28 IU/dL (ratio 0.93), FVIII 40 IU/dL, normal multimers.", "choice_c": "VWF antigen 90 IU/dL, VWF activity 35 IU/dL (ratio 0.39), FVIII 85 IU/dL, HMW multimer loss.", "choice_d": "VWF antigen 80 IU/dL, VWF activity 78 IU/dL (ratio 0.98), FVIII 22 IU/dL, abnormal VWF:FVIII binding.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Type 2N (\"Normandy\") is characterized by defective VWF-FVIII binding, producing normal or near-normal VWF antigen and activity but disproportionately low FVIII. The VWF:FVIII binding assay is abnormal. Choice A is Type 3, choice C is Type 2A pattern (HMW loss with reduced activity/antigen ratio), and choice D is Type 1.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A patient with Type 2B VWD undergoing elective cholecystectomy has preoperative platelet count 95,000/\u03bcL and VWF activity 25 IU/dL. Which preoperative hemostatic strategy is most appropriate?", "choice_a": "Plasma-derived VWF/FVIII concentrate at 40 IU/kg with hematology guidance; avoid empiric DDAVP.", "choice_b": "Tranexamic acid IV as monotherapy.", "choice_c": "DDAVP 0.3 mcg/kg IV to raise both VWF and FVIII.", "choice_d": "Platelet transfusion to reach 150,000 followed by DDAVP.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "DDAVP is generally contraindicated in Type 2B because DDAVP-induced release of the abnormal gain-of-function VWF can worsen platelet aggregation and thrombocytopenia. Plasma-derived VWF concentrate is the recommended first-line hemostatic therapy, with adjunctive TXA for mucosal bleeding risk. TXA alone is inadequate for a major abdominal case.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Per the ASH 2021 recommendation for neuraxial anesthesia in obstetric VWD patients, what factor activity range does the guideline support?", "choice_a": "\u2265200 IU/dL required regardless of clinical situation", "choice_b": "50\u2013150 IU/dL (with many institutions targeting \u2265100 IU/dL as a more conservative practical threshold)", "choice_c": "30\u201350 IU/dL", "choice_d": "10\u201330 IU/dL", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The ASH 2021 conditional recommendation for neuraxial anesthesia in obstetric VWD is factor levels in the 50\u2013150 IU/dL range, with acknowledgment of very low certainty of evidence and no established minimum threshold. Contemporary institutional practice (Sharpe et al., 2024) has trended toward \u2265100 IU/dL as a more conservative practical target, particularly when recombinant VWF is used.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A 62-year-old man with Type 1 VWD has been receiving Humate-P 40 IU/kg IV q12h for postoperative hemostasis after knee arthroplasty. On postoperative day 3, his VWF activity is 88 IU/dL and FVIII is 320 IU/dL. What is the most appropriate action?", "choice_a": "Increase the dose to 60 IU/kg q12h because bleeding protection is inadequate.", "choice_b": "Continue current dosing to maintain hemostasis.", "choice_c": "Extend the dosing interval or reduce the dose to lower FVIII toward \u2264250 IU/dL and reduce VTE risk.", "choice_d": "Discontinue mechanical VTE prophylaxis given the elevated FVIII.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Repeated dosing of plasma-derived VWF/FVIII concentrate can push trough FVIII above 250\u2013300 IU/dL because endogenous FVIII production continues and adds to the exogenous supply. Elevated FVIII substantially increases venous thromboembolism risk, particularly after orthopedic surgery. Options include extending the dosing interval, reducing the dose, or switching to Vonvendi (no FVIII content). Mechanical VTE prophylaxis should be continued.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which laboratory assay best distinguishes Type 2A from Type 2M VWD?", "choice_a": "VWF antigen", "choice_b": "FVIII activity", "choice_c": "PT / aPTT", "choice_d": "Multimer analysis", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Type 2A shows loss of high-molecular-weight multimers on multimer analysis; Type 2M shows preserved multimers despite reduced platelet or collagen binding function. Both have activity/antigen ratios <0.7 (qualitative defects), so multimer analysis is the discriminating test. PT/aPTT are insensitive to VWD subtype.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "A patient with type 1 VWD is scheduled for elective septoplasty. The hematology plan calls for DDAVP 0.3 mcg/kg IV 30 minutes preoperatively plus tranexamic acid 1 g IV at induction. Which additional postoperative order best reduces the risk of delayed mucosal bleeding?", "choice_a": "Oral tranexamic acid 25 mg/kg (approximately 1,300 mg) PO three times daily for 5\u20137 days.", "choice_b": "Ibuprofen 400 mg PO every 6 hours as needed.", "choice_c": "Aspirin 81 mg PO daily for 7 days.", "choice_d": "Warfarin 5 mg PO daily for 30 days.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Postoperative oral TXA at the ASH-recommended dose reduces delayed mucosal rebleeding, which typically occurs 24\u201372 hours postoperatively as the initial fibrin clot begins to dissolve and DDAVP effect wanes. Aspirin, NSAIDs, and warfarin all increase bleeding risk and are contraindicated.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "A patient with Type 3 VWD receives Vonvendi 50 IU/kg IV for urgent appendectomy. Baseline FVIII is 8 IU/dL. What is the most important additional consideration?", "choice_a": "No FVIII correction is needed because VWF replacement alone is sufficient.", "choice_b": "Vonvendi does not contain FVIII; pair with recombinant FVIII to achieve immediate FVIII correction because endogenous FVIII rise takes 6\u201312 hours.", "choice_c": "Vonvendi will immediately raise FVIII to the therapeutic range within 30 minutes.", "choice_d": "Give tranexamic acid contraindication check because TXA cannot be used with Vonvendi.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Vonvendi is recombinant VWF only and does not contain FVIII. In elective surgery it can be given 12\u201324 h before incision to allow endogenous FVIII to rise. In urgent surgery with low baseline FVIII (as in Type 3), Vonvendi must be paired with recombinant FVIII to achieve immediate correction of both parameters. TXA is compatible with Vonvendi.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which preoperative bleeding assessment score, using the ISTH Bleeding Assessment Tool (ISTH-BAT), should prompt further laboratory workup in an adult woman with a history suggestive of VWD?", "choice_a": "\u22653", "choice_b": "\u226510", "choice_c": "\u22656", "choice_d": "\u22652", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Per the ASH 2021 diagnosis guideline, an ISTH-BAT score of \u22654 in an adult male, \u22656 in an adult female, or \u22653 in a child is considered positive and prompts targeted laboratory testing for VWD. The higher female threshold reflects menstruation as a common bleeding source that does not itself indicate a disorder.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "During elective total knee arthroplasty in a patient with known Type 1 VWD (received DDAVP 0.3 mcg/kg IV 30 minutes before incision), the CRNA notices diffuse oozing at the surgical site and from the intravenous cannulation sites. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "choice_a": "Deepen the anesthetic with volatile agent to lower blood pressure and reduce bleeding.", "choice_b": "Give a second DDAVP dose 4 hours after the first for additive VWF release.", "choice_c": "Give ketorolac 30 mg IV for analgesia and to reduce inflammation.", "choice_d": "Reassess temperature, pH, ionized calcium, and platelet count; confirm DDAVP was delivered on time; and consider VWF-containing concentrate plus IV tranexamic acid.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The unexpected-bleeding action bundle begins with reassessment of the physiologic contributors to coagulopathy (temperature, pH, calcium) and confirmation that planned hemostatic therapy was actually given at the intended time. Adding VWF-containing concentrate and IV TXA addresses the specific VWD defect. Repeat DDAVP within 24 hours yields little additional benefit (tachyphylaxis). NSAIDs are contraindicated. Lowering blood pressure alone does not address a hemostatic defect.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which of the following is the most important element of the postoperative handoff for a patient with Type 3 VWD after major abdominal surgery?", "choice_a": "The subtype, product administered, next scheduled dose, laboratory monitoring plan, signs of concern, and hematology contact.", "choice_b": "The patient's dietary preferences.", "choice_c": "The exact intraoperative volume of intravenous crystalloid.", "choice_d": "The specific brand of surgical drain used.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The CRNA handoff for a VWD patient after major surgery must specify the subtype, therapies administered (product, dose, timing), the next scheduled dose, laboratory monitoring intervals, signs of concern (delayed bleeding, hematuria, calf swelling for VTE), and hematology contact information. This continuity is essential to avoid missed doses and to recognize delayed bleeding or thrombosis promptly.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "A term parturient with Type 3 VWD has been receiving wilate 40 IU/kg IV q12h beginning 24 hours before scheduled cesarean delivery, with a target VWF and FVIII \u2265100 IU/dL for neuraxial placement. Just before spinal placement, the current VWF activity is 62 IU/dL and FVIII is 48 IU/dL. What is the most appropriate action?", "choice_a": "Cancel the case indefinitely.", "choice_b": "Give an additional wilate dose to reach institutional target \u2265100 IU/dL before neuraxial placement, or convert to general anesthesia.", "choice_c": "Place the spinal anyway \u2014 62 IU/dL is within the ASH guideline range.", "choice_d": "Give DDAVP 0.3 mcg/kg IV to boost endogenous VWF and proceed.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The institutional target of \u2265100 IU/dL has not been met, and neuraxial placement below the planned threshold increases hematoma risk. Appropriate responses are to give an additional loading dose of wilate to reach target or to convert to general anesthesia. DDAVP is not effective in Type 3 (no endogenous stores). The case need not be cancelled \u2014 the obstetric indication remains.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of the following is a specific contraindication to systemic tranexamic acid?", "choice_a": "Postpartum hemorrhage prophylaxis.", "choice_b": "Menorrhagia in a patient with VWD.", "choice_c": "Upper urinary tract bleeding (risk of clot retention and obstruction) or active venous thromboembolic disease.", "choice_d": "Tonsillectomy in a patient with Type 1 VWD.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "TXA is contraindicated in upper urinary tract bleeding because clot stabilization in the ureter or renal pelvis can cause obstructive uropathy, and in active thromboembolic disease because it opposes fibrinolysis. TXA is specifically recommended for menorrhagia, postpartum hemorrhage, and mucosal procedures such as tonsillectomy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A patient with severe aortic stenosis and diagnosed Heyde syndrome (acquired VWS) is scheduled for TAVR. Which statement about postoperative acquired VWS is most accurate?", "choice_a": "Postoperative DDAVP must be given daily for 30 days to prevent recurrence.", "choice_b": "The acquired VWS is permanent and will require lifelong VWF replacement.", "choice_c": "The acquired VWS will worsen after TAVR because prosthetic materials increase shear stress.", "choice_d": "TAVR reliably reverses the acquired VWS within 24\u201372 hours as valvular shear stress normalizes and HMW multimers recover.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "TAVR (and surgical AVR) reliably reverses Heyde-syndrome acquired VWS within 24\u201372 hours as the shear stress across the valve normalizes, allowing HMW multimers to recover. This is why preoperative VWF concentrate is often not required for elective TAVR in Heyde syndrome \u2014 the definitive treatment removes the underlying mechanism. GI angiodysplasia bleeding also typically resolves.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_4", "question_id": "course_2_module_4_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which of the following situations most urgently warrants delaying elective surgery to obtain a hematology consultation and documented hemostatic plan?", "choice_a": "A patient with new-onset severe menorrhagia, ISTH-BAT score 8, mildly prolonged aPTT, and family history of postpartum hemorrhage, scheduled for hysterectomy in one week.", "choice_b": "A patient with no bleeding history and normal PT/aPTT.", "choice_c": "A patient with well-controlled hypertension scheduled for carpal tunnel release.", "choice_d": "A patient with known Type 1 VWD, prior successful DDAVP-covered dental extraction, scheduled for repeat dental extraction.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The presentation strongly suggests undiagnosed VWD (positive ISTH-BAT, prolonged aPTT that may reflect low FVIII, family history) and the planned operation is major with substantial mucosal bleeding risk. Proceeding without a documented VWF/FVIII workup and hematology plan risks major perioperative hemorrhage. The other scenarios do not require delay.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_5": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which receptor is considered the primary mediator of the clinical features of serotonin syndrome?", "choice_a": "5-HT2A", "choice_b": "5-HT4", "choice_c": "5-HT3", "choice_d": "5-HT1B", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "5-HT2A is the dominant receptor driving neuromuscular excitation, autonomic hyperactivity, and altered mental status in serotonin syndrome. 5-HT1A contributes but is not primary. 5-HT3 is the antiemetic target (ondansetron). 5-HT4 is a GI prokinetic receptor.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A patient on chronic sertraline is scheduled for parathyroidectomy. Which intraoperative agent poses the highest risk of precipitating serotonin syndrome?", "choice_a": "Sevoflurane", "choice_b": "Methylene blue", "choice_c": "Rocuronium", "choice_d": "Cefazolin", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Methylene blue is a potent MAO-A inhibitor and is a well-documented cause of intraoperative serotonin syndrome in patients on SSRIs, SNRIs, or MAOIs. The other agents are not serotonergic.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which examination finding is most useful for distinguishing serotonin syndrome from neuroleptic malignant syndrome?", "choice_a": "Lead-pipe rigidity", "choice_b": "Diaphoresis", "choice_c": "Inducible clonus and hyperreflexia", "choice_d": "Bradyreflexia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Inducible clonus and hyperreflexia are hallmark features of serotonin syndrome and are typically absent in NMS. NMS characteristically shows lead-pipe rigidity and bradyreflexia. Diaphoresis can occur in both syndromes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A patient on phenelzine is scheduled for cholecystectomy. Which analgesic is absolutely contraindicated?", "choice_a": "Hydromorphone", "choice_b": "Acetaminophen", "choice_c": "Morphine", "choice_d": "Meperidine", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "MAOI + meperidine is an absolute contraindication and one of the most dangerous drug combinations in perioperative medicine. Morphine and hydromorphone are the generally preferred opioids for MAOI patients. Acetaminophen is not serotonergic.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "According to the Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria, serotonin toxicity is diagnosed when a patient has taken a serotonergic agent AND:", "choice_a": "Any single criterion (e.g., spontaneous clonus alone) is met", "choice_b": "All five criteria are simultaneously met", "choice_c": "Both clonus and fever above 39\u00b0C are present", "choice_d": "At least three criteria are met", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The Hunter criteria diagnose serotonin toxicity when serotonergic exposure is present and any one criterion is met \u2014 including spontaneous clonus alone. The five-criterion hurdle is not required.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which of the following is the first-line pharmacologic treatment for agitation and neuromuscular hyperactivity in serotonin syndrome?", "choice_a": "Cyproheptadine", "choice_b": "Benzodiazepines", "choice_c": "Dantrolene", "choice_d": "Haloperidol", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Benzodiazepines (lorazepam, midazolam) are first-line for agitation, tremor, and autonomic surges. Cyproheptadine is adjunctive and enteral only. Haloperidol is a dopamine antagonist and should be avoided. Dantrolene is the treatment for malignant hyperthermia, not serotonin syndrome.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Why are antipyretics such as acetaminophen ineffective for the hyperthermia of serotonin syndrome?", "choice_a": "The fever is not centrally mediated", "choice_b": "They are inactivated by serotonergic drugs", "choice_c": "The hyperthermia is caused by muscular activity, not hypothalamic set-point change", "choice_d": "They cause hepatotoxicity in this setting", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Hyperthermia in serotonin syndrome is generated by increased muscular activity (tremor, clonus, rigidity). Antipyretics work by resetting a hypothalamic set-point, which is not the mechanism here. Active external cooling and benzodiazepine sedation (with paralysis when severe) are effective.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "In severe serotonin syndrome with hyperthermia above 40\u00b0C and marked rigidity requiring intubation, which neuromuscular blocker should generally be avoided?", "choice_a": "Vecuronium", "choice_b": "Cisatracurium", "choice_c": "Rocuronium", "choice_d": "Succinylcholine", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Succinylcholine is generally avoided in severe serotonin syndrome because hyperkalemia and rhabdomyolysis may already be present. Nondepolarizing agents (rocuronium, vecuronium, cisatracurium) are preferred.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "A patient on duloxetine is admitted to PACU after knee arthroplasty. The team plans opioid analgesia. Which opioid is generally the safer choice?", "choice_a": "Morphine", "choice_b": "Methadone", "choice_c": "Tramadol", "choice_d": "Meperidine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Morphine (and hydromorphone) have minimal serotonergic activity and are generally preferred in patients on chronic SSRI/SNRI. Tramadol, meperidine, and methadone all have serotonergic activity and increased risk of precipitating toxicity.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which perioperative antibiotic has weak, reversible MAO-A inhibitor activity and can precipitate serotonin syndrome in patients on SSRIs?", "choice_a": "Cefazolin", "choice_b": "Linezolid", "choice_c": "Vancomycin", "choice_d": "Piperacillin-tazobactam", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Linezolid is a weak, reversible MAO-A inhibitor and is an emerging perioperative trigger for serotonin syndrome. Cefazolin, vancomycin, and piperacillin-tazobactam do not carry serotonergic risk.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "The 2026 Zhang et al. multinational pharmacovigilance study identified the strongest opioid\u2013antidepressant signals for serotonin syndrome in which combinations?", "choice_a": "Morphine + SSRI and hydromorphone + SNRI", "choice_b": "Codeine + TCA and oxycodone + MAOI", "choice_c": "Tramadol + SSRI/SNRI and fentanyl + SSRI/SNRI", "choice_d": "Buprenorphine + SSRI and remifentanil + SNRI", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The Zhang et al. 2026 pharmacovigilance analysis identified SSRI/SNRI + tramadol and SSRI/SNRI + fentanyl as the strongest opioid\u2013antidepressant signals. Morphine and hydromorphone showed lower signals.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which finding on physical exam is most suggestive of serotonin syndrome rather than malignant hyperthermia?", "choice_a": "Masseter spasm after succinylcholine", "choice_b": "Rising end-tidal CO2", "choice_c": "Generalized muscle rigidity without clonus", "choice_d": "Bilateral lower-extremity clonus and hyperreflexia", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Clonus and hyperreflexia \u2014 particularly in the lower extremities \u2014 are hallmark features of serotonin syndrome and are absent in MH. MH characteristically shows rising ETCO2, masseter spasm, and diffuse rigidity without clonus.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which drug should generally be avoided as an antiemetic adjunct in a patient with an MAOI?", "choice_a": "Metoclopramide", "choice_b": "Aprepitant", "choice_c": "Dexamethasone", "choice_d": "Scopolamine patch", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Metoclopramide has central serotonergic and dopaminergic activity and adds serotonergic burden in an MAOI patient. Dexamethasone, scopolamine, and aprepitant are safer PONV alternatives.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "A patient on fluoxetine is being switched to an MAOI. What is the recommended washout period before starting the MAOI?", "choice_a": "1 week", "choice_b": "At least 5 weeks", "choice_c": "2 weeks", "choice_d": "3 days", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Fluoxetine's active metabolite norfluoxetine has a half-life of 4\u201316 days, so a washout of at least 5 weeks (sometimes longer) is recommended before starting an MAOI. Most other SSRIs require 1\u20132 weeks.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Which of the following statements about cyproheptadine is most accurate?", "choice_a": "It is available as an IV formulation for rapid use", "choice_b": "It is the first-line treatment for serotonin syndrome", "choice_c": "Evidence is largely limited to case reports and low-quality observational data; it is adjunctive to supportive care", "choice_d": "It is contraindicated when benzodiazepines have been given", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Per the 2020 Jacobs et al. systematic review and the 2024 Chiew and Isbister review, cyproheptadine evidence is low-quality; it is adjunctive to supportive care. It is enteral-only (no IV form) and is not contraindicated with benzodiazepines.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A 45-year-old on paroxetine develops agitation, diaphoresis, temperature 38.7\u00b0C, HR 130, BP 175/95, and inducible ankle clonus 30 minutes after receiving tramadol. The next best step is:", "choice_a": "Administer acetaminophen 1 g IV", "choice_b": "Administer physostigmine 1 mg IV", "choice_c": "Administer dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV", "choice_d": "Stop all serotonergic agents, give lorazepam 2 mg IV, and initiate active cooling", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Discontinuation of serotonergic agents, benzodiazepine sedation, and active external cooling are the foundation of management. Acetaminophen does not treat serotonin-syndrome hyperthermia. Dantrolene is for MH. Physostigmine is for anticholinergic toxicity.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of these clinical findings favors anticholinergic toxicity over serotonin syndrome?", "choice_a": "Dry skin, urinary retention, and ileus", "choice_b": "Lower-extremity hyperreflexia", "choice_c": "Inducible clonus", "choice_d": "Diaphoresis and hyperactive bowel sounds", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Anticholinergic toxicity classically shows dry skin, dry mucous membranes, urinary retention, and ileus. Serotonin syndrome typically shows diaphoresis and hyperactive bowel sounds along with clonus and hyperreflexia.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which is a reasonable dose of oral or NG cyproheptadine for adjunctive therapy in serotonin syndrome?", "choice_a": "100 mg once", "choice_b": "12 mg loading, 2 mg every 2 h as needed (max 32 mg/24 h)", "choice_c": "1 mg loading, 0.5 mg every 12 h", "choice_d": "50 mg loading, 25 mg every 6 h", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A commonly used cyproheptadine regimen is 12 mg PO/NG loading, then 2 mg every 2 hours as needed until clinical response, with a maximum of 32 mg in 24 hours. It is oral or NG only; there is no IV formulation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A patient on chronic sertraline is scheduled for elective surgery. What is the generally recommended approach to the sertraline?", "choice_a": "Double the sertraline dose the morning of surgery", "choice_b": "Switch to fluoxetine one week before surgery", "choice_c": "Continue sertraline through the perioperative period and avoid additive serotonergic perioperative triggers", "choice_d": "Stop sertraline 5 days before surgery", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Chronic SSRI therapy is generally continued through elective surgery because abrupt discontinuation carries withdrawal and mood risk. The safe strategy is to avoid additive perioperative triggers (methylene blue, meperidine, tramadol, linezolid).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which agent is described in the 2025 Lopez et al. case report as a useful adjunct for refractory postoperative serotonin syndrome?", "choice_a": "Ketamine infusion", "choice_b": "Naloxone infusion", "choice_c": "Bromocriptine", "choice_d": "Dexmedetomidine infusion", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The 2025 Lopez et al. Cureus case report described dexmedetomidine as a useful adjunct for refractory postoperative serotonin syndrome. It does not replace benzodiazepines, cooling, or paralysis but provides sympatholysis and sedation without respiratory depression.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "In severe serotonin syndrome with sustained hypertension despite adequate sedation, which is the preferred antihypertensive approach?", "choice_a": "Short-acting IV agents such as esmolol or nicardipine", "choice_b": "Reserpine", "choice_c": "Long-acting oral labetalol", "choice_d": "Sublingual nifedipine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Short-acting IV agents (esmolol, nicardipine, nitroprusside) allow titration and avoid hypotensive overshoot as the syndrome resolves. Long-acting, cumulative, or hard-to-reverse agents should be avoided.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "What is the most common time of clinical recognition of serotonin syndrome that begins intraoperatively?", "choice_a": "On postoperative day 3", "choice_b": "At emergence and in early PACU, when neuromuscular blockade reverses and depth lightens", "choice_c": "Immediately after induction", "choice_d": "During the middle of the surgical case", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Emergence and early PACU are the most common times of recognition because clonus, hyperreflexia, and agitation become detectable as blockade reverses and anesthetic depth lightens. Reassess neurologic exam specifically at emergence in any exposed patient.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT a Hunter criterion?", "choice_a": "Tremor plus hyperreflexia", "choice_b": "Ocular clonus plus agitation or diaphoresis", "choice_c": "Elevated creatine kinase above 5000 U/L", "choice_d": "Spontaneous clonus", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The Hunter criteria are entirely clinical \u2014 CK is used only for severity assessment and complication screening, not for diagnosis. The four correct Hunter criteria include spontaneous clonus, inducible clonus plus agitation/diaphoresis, ocular clonus plus agitation/diaphoresis, tremor plus hyperreflexia, and hypertonia plus fever plus clonus.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which laboratory abnormality is the most common serious complication of severe serotonin syndrome and drives fluid management?", "choice_a": "Hypothyroidism", "choice_b": "Hypoglycemia", "choice_c": "Hypercalcemia", "choice_d": "Rhabdomyolysis with elevated CK and risk of AKI", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Rhabdomyolysis from sustained muscular hyperactivity is the most common serious complication and drives aggressive IV crystalloid resuscitation to preserve renal function. Monitor CK, electrolytes, renal function, and urinalysis for myoglobin.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_5", "question_id": "course_2_module_5_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "A CRNA is planning shivering treatment in a PACU patient on phenelzine. Which agent should be avoided?", "choice_a": "Meperidine", "choice_b": "Clonidine", "choice_c": "Warm forced-air blanket", "choice_d": "Small-dose morphine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Meperidine is absolutely contraindicated with an MAOI. Warm forced-air blankets, small-dose morphine, and clonidine are acceptable alternatives for shivering treatment in the MAOI patient.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_6": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which three elements comprise the surgical fire triad?", "choice_a": "Oxidizer, ignition source, and fuel", "choice_b": "Electrocautery, drapes, and alcohol", "choice_c": "Oxygen, water, and heat", "choice_d": "Ignition, combustion, and propagation", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Every surgical fire requires an oxidizer (oxygen and/or nitrous oxide), an ignition source (electrocautery, laser, fiberoptic light, drill), and a fuel (drapes, sponges, alcohol prep, tube, hair). Removing any one element prevents or extinguishes the fire.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "The CRNA's single most modifiable fire-risk lever is:", "choice_a": "Table height and drape configuration", "choice_b": "Delivered oxygen concentration and use of nitrous oxide", "choice_c": "Choice of induction agent", "choice_d": "Selection of the tracheal tube brand", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The CRNA controls the oxidizer. Keeping open-delivery oxygen at \u226430% in high-risk cases and avoiding nitrous oxide (which supports combustion similarly to oxygen) are the two anesthesia-specific levers with the largest evidence base.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which case type is most consistently overrepresented in surgical fire closed-claims analyses?", "choice_a": "Laparoscopic abdominal surgery under general anesthesia", "choice_b": "Cardiac catheterization", "choice_c": "MAC with open oxygen delivery for head, face, or neck procedures", "choice_d": "Elective total knee arthroplasty", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Upper-body procedures (head, face, neck, upper chest) performed under MAC with open oxygen delivery are the dominant closed-claims scenario for surgical fires.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Electrosurgical devices are the ignition source in approximately what proportion of surgical fires?", "choice_a": "About 90%", "choice_b": "About one-half", "choice_c": "About one-third", "choice_d": "About two-thirds", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Historical ECRI and closed-claims data attribute roughly two-thirds of surgical fires to electrosurgery, with lasers accounting for most of the remainder.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "ChloraPrep contains:", "choice_a": "2% chlorhexidine gluconate with 70% isopropyl alcohol", "choice_b": "Povidone-iodine 10% aqueous", "choice_c": "2% chlorhexidine gluconate in aqueous solution", "choice_d": "Iodine povacrylex with 74% isopropyl alcohol", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "ChloraPrep is 2% chlorhexidine gluconate combined with 70% isopropyl alcohol. DuraPrep is the iodine povacrylex/74% isopropanol product.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Minimum ChloraPrep drying time on hairless skin per the manufacturer IFU is:", "choice_a": "10 minutes", "choice_b": "3 minutes", "choice_c": "30 seconds", "choice_d": "1 minute", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "ChloraPrep requires a minimum of 3 minutes to dry on hairless skin and up to 1 hour in hair-bearing areas. Draping or ignition-source activation before drying risks vapor ignition.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Maximum time that hair-bearing areas may require for ChloraPrep to dry completely is:", "choice_a": "15 minutes", "choice_b": "30 minutes", "choice_c": "Up to 1 hour", "choice_d": "5 minutes", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Hair-bearing areas may require up to 1 hour for alcohol-based preps to dry completely; wet hair remains flammable.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which statement about aqueous povidone-iodine is correct?", "choice_a": "It carries the same alcohol-vapor flammability warning as ChloraPrep", "choice_b": "It cannot be used in the OR", "choice_c": "It requires no drying time at all", "choice_d": "It does not carry the same alcohol-vapor flammability warning, but should still be allowed to dry per labeling", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Aqueous povidone-iodine does not carry the alcohol-vapor flammability warning that alcohol-based preps do, but manufacturers typically still specify a drying period before draping.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "In a high-risk case with open oxygen delivery, the widely referenced FiO2 target is:", "choice_a": "\u226430% when clinically feasible", "choice_b": "No specific target has been described", "choice_c": "100% for maximum reserve", "choice_d": "50% at all times", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "APSF and ASA guidance recommend targeting open-delivery FiO2 at \u226430% during high-risk cases when clinically feasible.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "When higher oxygen concentrations than 30% are clinically necessary during a high-risk case, the recommended alternative is to:", "choice_a": "Continue high-concentration open delivery", "choice_b": "Secure the airway with an LMA or cuffed endotracheal tube", "choice_c": "Increase nitrous oxide instead", "choice_d": "Turn off the pulse oximeter", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "If oxygen requirements exceed the 30% open-delivery target, securing the airway (LMA or cuffed ETT) allows delivery of the required FiO2 without an oxidizer-enriched surgical field.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Why is nitrous oxide avoided in high-fire-risk cases?", "choice_a": "It causes hyperthermia", "choice_b": "It is contraindicated with all inhalational agents", "choice_c": "It supports combustion in a manner similar to oxygen", "choice_d": "It irritates the airway", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Nitrous oxide is an oxidizer that supports combustion similarly to oxygen. A 50/50 oxygen/nitrous oxide environment behaves similarly to a 100% oxygen environment with respect to ignition energy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which of the following belongs in the time-out fire risk assessment?", "choice_a": "The circulator's break schedule", "choice_b": "The patient's blood type", "choice_c": "The surgeon's preferred coffee order", "choice_d": "Confirmation that alcohol-based prep is dry, expected use of ignition sources, and current oxidizer status", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "A hazard-based time-out fire assessment confirms the presence and status of each triad element: prep-dry status, oxidizer status, and planned ignition source use.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Modern hazard-based fire risk assessments are preferred over simple low/medium/high scoring because:", "choice_a": "They identify which specific triad elements are present and trigger the specific prevention steps required", "choice_b": "They generate more billing", "choice_c": "They eliminate the need for the surgical time-out", "choice_d": "They are required by CMS as a Never Event", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Hazard-based assessments (per Joint Commission 2024 guidance) identify the specific triad elements in play and trigger the corresponding prevention steps, rather than assigning a single categorical score.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "If a fire occurs in the OR, the CRNA's priority action is to:", "choice_a": "Push emergency drugs", "choice_b": "Discontinue supplemental oxygen and nitrous oxide and, when indicated, disconnect the breathing circuit", "choice_c": "Extubate the patient immediately", "choice_d": "Call the fire department", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Removing the oxidizer is the anesthesia professional's immediate action. Discontinuing oxygen and nitrous oxide and disconnecting the circuit when indicated eliminates the CRNA's element of the triad.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "The correct immediate sequence for a known airway fire is:", "choice_a": "Increase FiO2, then extubate", "choice_b": "Continue ventilation on 100% oxygen to protect against hypoxemia", "choice_c": "Disconnect the circuit and stop all airway gases, remove the tube, pour saline into the airway, then re-establish ventilation", "choice_d": "Call for a code cart before touching the airway", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Airway fires require immediate cessation of gas flow (disconnect the circuit), removal of the involved tube, saline irrigation of the airway to extinguish and cool tissue, and then re-establishment of ventilation on the lowest feasible FiO2.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "For a small fire on the patient or in the surgical field, the preferred initial extinguishing agent is:", "choice_a": "Water from the scrub sink", "choice_b": "A CO2 extinguisher as the first step", "choice_c": "A dry chemical extinguisher", "choice_d": "Saline or sterile water", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Saline or sterile water is the preferred initial extinguishing agent for small fires on the patient or in the airway. A CO2 extinguisher is used if the fire persists or escalates.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "After an airway fire has been extinguished, which of the following is commonly indicated?", "choice_a": "Bronchoscopy to evaluate for retained tube fragments, soot, mucosal injury, and edema", "choice_b": "Head CT", "choice_c": "Cardiac catheterization", "choice_d": "Immediate discharge home", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Bronchoscopy is commonly indicated after airway fires to identify retained tracheal tube fragments, soot, mucosal burns, and edema that may progress to airway compromise.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "In the OR, the RACE model (Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish/Evacuate):", "choice_a": "Replaces the seven-step surgical fire response for on-patient and airway fires", "choice_b": "Is the broad facility fire framework; the immediate surgical fire response takes priority for on-patient and airway fires", "choice_c": "Is the primary algorithm for airway fires", "choice_d": "Applies only to nursing homes", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "RACE is the hospital-wide fire framework. For a fire on the patient or in the airway, the immediate surgical fire response (announce, stop oxidizer, stop ignition, remove fuel, extinguish, re-establish ventilation, debrief) takes priority; the team continues with RACE actions once the immediate patient threat is controlled.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "The PASS mnemonic for fire extinguisher use stands for:", "choice_a": "Push, Aim, Spray, Stop", "choice_b": "Prepare, Alert, Spray, Suppress", "choice_c": "Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep", "choice_d": "Point, Activate, Spray, Stop", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "PASS: Pull the pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle, Sweep from side to side.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Occlusive drape configurations increase fire risk because they:", "choice_a": "Reduce patient warming", "choice_b": "Increase risk of pressure injury", "choice_c": "Interfere with hemostasis", "choice_d": "Trap oxygen at or near the surgical site, creating an oxidizer-enriched microenvironment", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Occlusive drapes can trap oxygen delivered by nasal cannula or face mask, creating an oxidizer-enriched microenvironment adjacent to the ignition source. Suction scavenging under drapes is one mitigation strategy per local policy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "For a CO2 laser microlaryngoscopy case, the CRNA-specific safeguards include which of the following?", "choice_a": "Laser-resistant tube, saline-filled cuff (with methylene blue per IFU), FiO2 \u226430% when clinically feasible, and avoidance of N2O", "choice_b": "100% FiO2 and standard PVC tube", "choice_c": "Bipolar cautery only", "choice_d": "Nitrous oxide 70% to keep FiO2 at 30%", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Airway laser cases require a laser-resistant endotracheal tube, saline-filled cuff (methylene blue makes rupture visible, per IFU), the lowest feasible FiO2 (commonly \u226430%), and avoidance of nitrous oxide.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following is a correct rule for ignition-device handling?", "choice_a": "The circulator may activate the electrocautery on behalf of the surgeon", "choice_b": "The person activating the ignition device should be the person using it, and inactive devices should remain in a holster or standby mode", "choice_c": "Ignition devices may rest on the surgical drapes when not in active use", "choice_d": "Fiberoptic light cords may be disconnected while the light source is on", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The user activates the ignition device, and inactive devices are placed in a holster or standby mode. Fiberoptic light cords are connected before the light source is activated, and hot tips are kept off drapes and sponges.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "The Joint Commission Sentinel Event Alert 68 (2023\u20132024 update) emphasizes which of the following?", "choice_a": "Eliminating alcohol-based preps in favor of soap and water", "choice_b": "Mandatory 100% FiO2 for all cases", "choice_c": "Hospital-wide fire prevention programs, hazard-based time-out assessment, oxygen management, prep drying-time enforcement, and structured debrief after fires and near misses", "choice_d": "Discontinuing the surgical time-out", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Sentinel Event Alert 68 (2024) emphasizes program-level and case-level actions: written programs, hazard-based time-out assessment, oxygen management, prep drying enforcement, and structured debrief.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Within the first 10 seconds of a surgical fire, the team's priority actions include:", "choice_a": "Complete documentation", "choice_b": "Call for consultation", "choice_c": "Prepare for evacuation", "choice_d": "Announce \"fire,\" stop the procedure, discontinue O2 and N2O, and stop the ignition source", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The 0\u201310 second priorities are announcement, procedure stop, oxidizer discontinuation, and ignition-source deactivation. Fuel removal, extinguishment, and ventilation re-establishment follow in the next 50 seconds.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_6", "question_id": "course_2_module_6_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "After any surgical fire or near miss, best practice is to:", "choice_a": "Document the event thoroughly, report through institutional channels, preserve implicated equipment, and conduct a structured debrief with system-level improvement", "choice_b": "Blame the surgical technologist", "choice_c": "Assign additional cases to the same room to test the equipment", "choice_d": "Move on and forget the event", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Post-event best practice is thorough documentation, institutional reporting, equipment preservation for biomedical review, and a structured debrief that identifies contributing factors and drives system change. Regular fire drills and simulation build the reliable performance needed in the first 60 seconds of a real event.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_7": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Negative pressure pulmonary edema (NPPE) is best described as:", "choice_a": "A hydrostatic noncardiogenic pulmonary edema caused by markedly negative intrathoracic pressure", "choice_b": "A drug-induced pulmonary edema from opioid administration", "choice_c": "A permeability pulmonary edema caused by systemic inflammation", "choice_d": "A cardiogenic pulmonary edema due to left ventricular failure", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "NPPE is primarily a hydrostatic noncardiogenic pulmonary edema produced by very negative intrathoracic pressures generated during inspiration against an obstructed upper airway.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A healthy adult attempting to inhale against a closed glottis can commonly generate intrathoracic pressures in what range?", "choice_a": "-200 to -300 cm H2O", "choice_b": "-50 to -100 cm H2O", "choice_c": "-15 to -25 cm H2O", "choice_d": "-5 to -10 cm H2O", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Approximately -50 to -100 cm H2O is the commonly cited range that can drive NPPE, though susceptibility depends on obstruction severity, duration, and patient factors.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which perioperative event is the classic trigger of Type I NPPE?", "choice_a": "Massive transfusion", "choice_b": "Pulmonary embolism during positioning", "choice_c": "Laryngospasm after extubation", "choice_d": "Anaphylaxis to rocuronium", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Type I NPPE most often follows an acute obstruction such as laryngospasm after extubation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Type II NPPE typically occurs after:", "choice_a": "Rapid infusion of crystalloid", "choice_b": "Prolonged mechanical ventilation with high PEEP", "choice_c": "Neuromuscular blockade with sugammadex reversal", "choice_d": "Sudden relief of a chronic upper airway obstruction (e.g., adult tonsillectomy for OSA)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Type II NPPE follows sudden relief of chronic upper airway obstruction, when altered airway dynamics and intrathoracic pressures favor edema formation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which patient is at the highest classic risk for Type I NPPE based on Holmes et al.'s description?", "choice_a": "Young, muscular, athletic adult (often male) after post-extubation laryngospasm", "choice_b": "Patient with end-stage renal disease on dialysis", "choice_c": "Neonate after routine circumcision", "choice_d": "Frail elderly patient with heart failure", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Young, healthy, athletic adults \u2014 often male \u2014 can generate large negative intrathoracic pressures and were the group Holmes and colleagues classically described.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which finding is most characteristic of NPPE?", "choice_a": "Slow onset dyspnea over several days", "choice_b": "Sudden hypoxemia and frothy or pink sputum after an obstructive event", "choice_c": "Unilateral wheezing without hypoxemia", "choice_d": "Isolated hypercapnia with a clear chest", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Sudden hypoxemia, tachypnea, crackles, and frothy/pink sputum after an obstructive event are hallmark features of NPPE.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Chest imaging in NPPE typically shows:", "choice_a": "Pneumothorax with tracheal deviation", "choice_b": "Focal consolidation with cavitation", "choice_c": "Diffuse bilateral interstitial and alveolar infiltrates with a normal cardiac silhouette", "choice_d": "Massive pleural effusion", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Diffuse bilateral infiltrates with a normal cardiac silhouette are typical; a normal-sized heart helps distinguish NPPE from cardiogenic edema.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which is the most appropriate first intervention when NPPE is suspected in the PACU?", "choice_a": "Administer bronchodilators before assessing the airway", "choice_b": "Administer furosemide 40 mg IV", "choice_c": "Withhold oxygen to avoid oxygen toxicity", "choice_d": "Relieve any residual airway obstruction, apply 100% oxygen, and initiate positive-pressure support", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Treatment is airway-centered: relieve the obstruction, apply 100% oxygen, and provide positive-pressure support before considering pharmacologic adjuncts.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "The commonly cited dose of succinylcholine to break laryngospasm in an adult with IV access is:", "choice_a": "0.1\u20130.5 mg/kg IV", "choice_b": "1.5\u20132.5 mg/kg IV", "choice_c": "0.01\u20130.05 mg/kg IV", "choice_d": "5\u201310 mg/kg IV", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "A subparalytic to low paralytic dose of 0.1\u20130.5 mg/kg IV is commonly cited to break laryngospasm, with onset in about 30\u201360 seconds.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "When IV access is not available in a patient with persistent laryngospasm, succinylcholine may be given as:", "choice_a": "20 mg/kg IM", "choice_b": "4 mg/kg IM, maximum 150\u2013200 mg, with onset in approximately 2\u20133 minutes", "choice_c": "1 mg/kg subcutaneously", "choice_d": "0.1 mg/kg sublingually", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "4 mg/kg IM (max 150\u2013200 mg) is the commonly cited IM dose, with onset approximately 2\u20133 minutes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which adverse effect of succinylcholine is most important to consider before repeat dosing in a pediatric patient?", "choice_a": "Chronic constipation", "choice_b": "Peripheral neuropathy", "choice_c": "Vagally mediated bradycardia; consider atropine 0.02 mg/kg IV pretreatment when bradycardia risk is clinically important", "choice_d": "Hepatic failure", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Children are more susceptible to vagally mediated bradycardia during airway manipulation and after succinylcholine, especially with repeat dosing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "The FDA boxed warning for succinylcholine in pediatric patients relates to which catastrophic event?", "choice_a": "Fatal hepatic necrosis", "choice_b": "Delayed-onset renal failure", "choice_c": "Anaphylaxis at first exposure only", "choice_d": "Hyperkalemic rhabdomyolysis and cardiac arrest in children with undiagnosed skeletal muscle myopathy (e.g., Duchenne muscular dystrophy)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The boxed warning addresses rare but catastrophic hyperkalemic rhabdomyolysis and cardiac arrest in apparently healthy children later found to have undiagnosed myopathy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A patient with pseudocholinesterase deficiency who receives a standard intubating dose of succinylcholine should be expected to have:", "choice_a": "Prolonged paralysis lasting well beyond the usual duration", "choice_b": "No clinical effect", "choice_c": "Immediate resistance to succinylcholine", "choice_d": "Severe hypertension only", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Pseudocholinesterase deficiency slows the breakdown of succinylcholine, producing prolonged paralysis that requires continued ventilation until spontaneous recovery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Propofol is used in the laryngospasm\u2013NPPE cascade primarily to:", "choice_a": "Provide long-term sedation in the ICU", "choice_b": "Deepen anesthesia to abort partial laryngospasm and blunt airway reflexes before succinylcholine is required", "choice_c": "Diurese pulmonary edema", "choice_d": "Reverse residual neuromuscular blockade", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Propofol deepens anesthesia and blunts airway reflexes, often aborting partial laryngospasm before succinylcholine is needed.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A reasonable propofol rescue bolus to deepen anesthesia during laryngospasm in a hemodynamically stable adult is approximately:", "choice_a": "0.05 mg/kg IV", "choice_b": "5.0 mg/kg IV", "choice_c": "0.5\u20131.0 mg/kg IV", "choice_d": "10 mg/kg IV", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "0.5\u20131.0 mg/kg IV is a commonly used sub-anesthetic to low anesthetic rescue bolus that can abort partial laryngospasm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which statement about furosemide in NPPE is most accurate?", "choice_a": "Furosemide replaces the need for positive-pressure ventilation", "choice_b": "Furosemide is contraindicated in NPPE", "choice_c": "Furosemide should be given routinely to every patient with NPPE", "choice_d": "Furosemide is not the primary treatment; use selectively for concomitant volume overload, hypertension, or persistent edema after airway and ventilatory support", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "NPPE is driven by hydrostatic fluid shifts, not total-body volume excess. Use furosemide selectively; the definitive treatment is airway relief and positive-pressure support.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "The mainstay of NPPE treatment is:", "choice_a": "Positive airway pressure \u2014 CPAP or NIPPV for mild cases, endotracheal intubation with PEEP for severe cases", "choice_b": "High-dose corticosteroids", "choice_c": "Aggressive diuresis", "choice_d": "Bronchoalveolar lavage", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Positive airway pressure is the mainstay: CPAP or NIPPV for mild disease and intubation with PEEP for severe hypoxemia or high work of breathing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which is the correct FIRST step in the seven-step laryngospasm treatment algorithm?", "choice_a": "Call the ICU for admission", "choice_b": "Recognize the event early (stridor or silence, paradoxical chest movement, absent capnography, falling SpO2)", "choice_c": "Administer furosemide", "choice_d": "Administer succinylcholine", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Early recognition is step 1. Fast recognition allows immediate corrective steps before large negative intrathoracic pressures develop.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT part of the initial step 3 laryngospasm bundle (100% O2 + CPAP + jaw thrust)?", "choice_a": "Applying continuous positive airway pressure", "choice_b": "Applying 100% oxygen", "choice_c": "Administering IV furosemide", "choice_d": "Performing a vigorous jaw thrust (Larson maneuver)", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Furosemide is not part of the acute laryngospasm response. Step 3 is 100% O2, CPAP, and vigorous jaw thrust, with an oropharyngeal airway if appropriate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Most patients with NPPE improve substantially:", "choice_a": "Only after 7 to 10 days of mechanical ventilation", "choice_b": "Only after ECMO", "choice_c": "Within 1 to 2 hours if given furosemide", "choice_d": "Within 12 to 24 hours once the obstruction is relieved and supportive care is provided", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Substantial improvement within 12 to 24 hours after obstruction relief and supportive care is typical; disposition should still be based on sustained clinical improvement.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which item is NOT part of the classic differential diagnosis for acute post-extubation hypoxemia consistent with NPPE?", "choice_a": "Chronic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed years earlier", "choice_b": "Aspiration pneumonitis", "choice_c": "Cardiogenic pulmonary edema", "choice_d": "Anaphylaxis", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Chronic pulmonary fibrosis is a long-standing diagnosis and does not present as new acute post-extubation hypoxemia. The acute differential includes aspiration, cardiogenic edema, anaphylaxis, residual paralysis, bronchospasm, and pulmonary embolism.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which extubation strategy is most likely to reduce laryngospasm risk?", "choice_a": "Extubate during Stage 2 anesthesia", "choice_b": "Extubate awake with confirmed neuromuscular reversal (TOF ratio \u2265 0.9 or after sugammadex) and appropriate suctioning", "choice_c": "Extubate without suctioning to minimize stimulation", "choice_d": "Extubate before neuromuscular reversal to save time", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Awake extubation with confirmed reversal and appropriate suctioning reduces the risk of laryngospasm and airway obstruction that precedes NPPE. Stage 2 extubation should be avoided.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which patient group deserves heightened peri-extubation vigilance for NPPE?", "choice_a": "Only patients with heart failure", "choice_b": "Only patients under 20 years of age", "choice_c": "Patients with obesity, OSA, upper airway surgery, difficult airway, or young athletic adults", "choice_d": "Only patients with renal disease", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Obesity, OSA, upper airway surgery, known difficult airway, and young muscular adults are recognized higher-risk populations for NPPE.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Luo, Li, & Qin (2025, BMC Anesthesiology) reported that in selected high-risk populations such as rhinoplasty and spine surgery, the incidence of NPPE can reach:", "choice_a": "0.001%", "choice_b": "0.05%", "choice_c": "Universally 50%", "choice_d": "Approximately 9\u201311%", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Reported incidence in the general perioperative population is approximately 0.05\u20130.1%, but selected high-risk populations (rhinoplasty, spine surgery, adult tonsillectomy) can approach 9\u201311%.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_7", "question_id": "course_2_module_7_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "After successful management of NPPE in the PACU, ICU escalation is warranted when the patient:", "choice_a": "Requires reintubation, sustained PEEP, high oxygen requirements, or close hemodynamic and respiratory monitoring", "choice_b": "Has SpO2 98% on room air and is walking", "choice_c": "Refuses to eat breakfast", "choice_d": "Has a normal chest x-ray", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Reintubation, ongoing PEEP, high oxygen requirements, or a need for close hemodynamic and respiratory monitoring are recognized ICU escalation criteria.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_8": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which combination of findings should raise the strongest suspicion for AFE?", "choice_a": "Sudden peripartum hypoxia, hypotension or cardiac arrest, and early coagulopathy", "choice_b": "Uterine tenderness with fever alone", "choice_c": "Isolated postpartum hypertension without other symptoms", "choice_d": "Mild postpartum fatigue and normal vital signs", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The classic AFE triad is acute hypoxia, hypotension or cardiac arrest, and coagulopathy occurring in the peripartum period.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "AFE is best characterized as:", "choice_a": "A definitive laboratory diagnosis", "choice_b": "A clinical diagnosis of exclusion", "choice_c": "A histopathologic-only diagnosis", "choice_d": "A radiologic diagnosis", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "AFE is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion; no lab test or imaging can confirm it in real time.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "The pathophysiologic cascade of AFE most commonly begins with:", "choice_a": "IgE-mediated anaphylaxis to fetal protein", "choice_b": "Direct viral injury to the alveolar epithelium", "choice_c": "A non-IgE anaphylactoid reaction with complement activation and mast cell degranulation after amniotic fluid components enter maternal circulation", "choice_d": "Acute bacterial endotoxemia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "AFE is best described as a non-IgE anaphylactoid reaction with complement activation and cytokine surge, not classical IgE anaphylaxis.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Phase 1 of AFE is dominated by:", "choice_a": "Insidious onset over several days", "choice_b": "Chronic hypertension and proteinuria", "choice_c": "Isolated hemorrhage without cardiovascular changes", "choice_d": "Acute pulmonary vasoconstriction, RV strain or failure, hypoxemia, and cardiovascular collapse", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Phase 1 features acute pulmonary hypertension, RV failure, hypoxemia, hypotension, and often cardiac arrest.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Phase 2 of AFE is dominated by:", "choice_a": "Hemostatic failure with DIC, LV dysfunction, pulmonary edema, and multiorgan hypoperfusion", "choice_b": "Isolated hypertension", "choice_c": "Chronic musculoskeletal pain", "choice_d": "Full recovery within minutes", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Phase 2 features DIC, LV dysfunction, pulmonary edema, and multiorgan hypoperfusion in survivors of the initial collapse.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Because pregnancy elevates baseline fibrinogen, a fibrinogen level of 2 g/L during suspected AFE should be interpreted as:", "choice_a": "Reassuring evidence against DIC", "choice_b": "A late and dangerous sign of severe obstetric hemostatic failure requiring immediate replacement", "choice_c": "Normal for pregnancy", "choice_d": "An indication to withhold cryoprecipitate", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Term parturients have fibrinogen typically 4\u20136 g/L. A value of 2 g/L reflects severe depletion and requires urgent fibrinogen replacement.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "The extrinsic coagulation pathway is activated in AFE primarily by:", "choice_a": "Autoimmune antibody formation", "choice_b": "Viral RNA in maternal serum", "choice_c": "Tissue factor released from amniotic fluid components entering the maternal circulation", "choice_d": "Bacterial endotoxin", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Amniotic fluid is rich in tissue factor, which activates the extrinsic pathway and drives rapid DIC.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "The single most important initial CRNA action when AFE is suspected is to:", "choice_a": "Order a chest CT before treatment", "choice_b": "Complete registry paperwork before starting resuscitation", "choice_c": "Wait for confirmatory laboratory testing before intervening", "choice_d": "Announce suspected AFE, call for help, and activate the obstetric emergency response while beginning airway, breathing, and circulation support", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Recognition, help, activation, and immediate ABC support are the priority; do not wait for confirmatory testing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "For a gravid patient with a fundus at or above the umbilicus who requires CPR, the correct pregnancy modification is:", "choice_a": "Continuous manual left uterine displacement with supine chest compressions", "choice_b": "Prone positioning", "choice_c": "Left lateral tilt at 30\u00b0 during compressions", "choice_d": "Withholding defibrillation because of pregnancy", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Current AHA guidance favors supine compressions with continuous manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem cesarean delivery) during maternal cardiac arrest primarily benefits:", "choice_a": "Neither the mother nor the neonate", "choice_b": "Both the mother and the neonate by improving venous return, reducing aortocaval compression, and increasing the likelihood of maternal ROSC", "choice_c": "The neonate only", "choice_d": "Only the mother", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Uterine evacuation improves venous return and ventilation mechanics, benefiting maternal resuscitation as well as neonatal survival.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "The vasopressor most likely to spare pulmonary vascular resistance and support the failing right ventricle in early AFE is:", "choice_a": "Nitroprusside", "choice_b": "Dopamine at renal-dose infusion", "choice_c": "Vasopressin", "choice_d": "Phenylephrine", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Vasopressin increases systemic vascular resistance with relatively less pulmonary vasoconstriction, which is helpful in RV failure with pulmonary hypertension.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Excessive crystalloid administration during AFE resuscitation is problematic because it can:", "choice_a": "Improve fibrinogen levels", "choice_b": "Reverse DIC", "choice_c": "Cure pulmonary hypertension", "choice_d": "Worsen RV dilation, pulmonary edema, tissue edema, and dilutional coagulopathy", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Excessive crystalloid worsens RV distension, edema, and dilutional coagulopathy; balanced blood products are preferred.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "The uterotonic that should be avoided in patients with severe hypertension is:", "choice_a": "Methylergonovine", "choice_b": "Misoprostol", "choice_c": "Oxytocin", "choice_d": "Calcium gluconate", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Methylergonovine can cause severe vasoconstriction and hypertension and is contraindicated in severe hypertension.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Rapid IV bolus of oxytocin should be avoided because it can cause:", "choice_a": "Severe hypertension", "choice_b": "Profound hypotension and tachycardia", "choice_c": "Bronchospasm", "choice_d": "Malignant hyperthermia", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Rapid oxytocin bolus causes profound systemic vasodilation, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia; use dilute infusion or slow controlled bolus.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Which uterotonic should generally be avoided in patients with reactive airway disease?", "choice_a": "Oxytocin", "choice_b": "Misoprostol", "choice_c": "Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2\u03b1)", "choice_d": "Methylergonovine", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Carboprost can cause bronchospasm and is generally avoided in reactive airway disease.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "For selected refractory acute pulmonary hypertension and RV failure during AFE, an appropriate inhaled pulmonary vasodilator option is:", "choice_a": "Inhaled magnesium", "choice_b": "Inhaled nitroprusside", "choice_c": "Inhaled succinylcholine", "choice_d": "Inhaled nitric oxide or inhaled epoprostenol", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Inhaled nitric oxide and inhaled epoprostenol are selective pulmonary vasodilators used in selected refractory pulmonary hypertension.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which laboratory pattern is most consistent with AFE-associated DIC?", "choice_a": "Rapidly falling fibrinogen, thrombocytopenia, prolonged PT and aPTT, elevated D-dimer", "choice_b": "Elevated fibrinogen, normal PT/aPTT, normal D-dimer", "choice_c": "Isolated leukocytosis without coagulation changes", "choice_d": "Hyperkalemia without other findings", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "AFE-associated DIC produces falling fibrinogen, thrombocytopenia, prolonged PT/aPTT, and elevated D-dimer.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Viscoelastic testing during AFE resuscitation is most useful because it:", "choice_a": "Replaces the need for clinical assessment", "choice_b": "Guides targeted replacement of fibrinogen, platelets, and plasma based on real-time functional clot data", "choice_c": "Determines fetal viability", "choice_d": "Confirms the diagnosis of AFE", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Viscoelastic testing does not diagnose AFE but guides targeted component therapy during massive transfusion.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which condition most closely mimics AFE by producing sudden peripartum collapse and can coexist with it?", "choice_a": "Postpartum blues", "choice_b": "Isolated bladder distension", "choice_c": "Massive obstetric hemorrhage", "choice_d": "Peripartum lactation mastitis", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Massive obstetric hemorrhage is a leading competing diagnosis and often coexists with AFE during the same crisis.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which finding would most argue AGAINST high or total spinal anesthesia as the cause of peripartum collapse?", "choice_a": "Ascending motor weakness", "choice_b": "Hypotension and bradycardia after neuraxial dosing", "choice_c": "Difficulty speaking after epidural bolus", "choice_d": "Early diffuse coagulopathy with fibrinogen depletion and surgical field oozing", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Early DIC and diffuse coagulopathy are not features of high spinal and should raise concern for AFE or another coagulopathic process.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) for AFE should typically be considered:", "choice_a": "Early, when severe hypoxemia, pulmonary hypertension, RV failure, or refractory shock persists despite optimized management, and an ECMO-capable team is available", "choice_b": "Never; it is contraindicated in AFE", "choice_c": "Only after all bleeding has permanently stopped", "choice_d": "Only if the diagnosis is confirmed by autopsy", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Early VA-ECMO consultation is reasonable for refractory cardiopulmonary failure in AFE, with recognition of bleeding and anticoagulation challenges.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "The safest way to manage AFE Registry specimen collection during active resuscitation is to:", "choice_a": "Ask the airway clinician to complete all registry tasks during intubation", "choice_b": "Delegate specimen preservation to a team member only after critical resuscitative actions are underway", "choice_c": "Pause resuscitation until specimens are collected", "choice_d": "Delay transfusion until research specimens are obtained", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Clinical care always takes priority; registry activities are delegated and must not interrupt airway, CPR, transfusion, or hemorrhage control.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "The 2024 Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine clinical guideline on AFE emphasizes all of the following EXCEPT:", "choice_a": "Multidisciplinary team activation and early aggressive resuscitation", "choice_b": "Clinical diagnosis of exclusion", "choice_c": "Delaying massive transfusion until a specific laboratory threshold is reached", "choice_d": "High-quality CPR and ACLS with pregnancy modifications", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "SMFM 2024 emphasizes early aggressive resuscitation and early massive transfusion when bleeding, shock, or coagulopathy is present \u2014 not delay for a lab threshold.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Stafford et al. (2020) analysis of the U.S. AFE International Registry reported that:", "choice_a": "AFE occurs only after cesarean delivery", "choice_b": "AFE is prevented by routine antenatal aspirin", "choice_c": "AFE was found to have no consistent clinical pattern", "choice_d": "AFE cases show recognizable patterns of acute cardiorespiratory compromise and coagulopathy, and registry data help refine diagnostic criteria and management", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Registry analyses have helped clarify the clinical syndrome and support refinement of diagnostic and management approaches.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_8", "question_id": "course_2_module_8_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Collins et al. (2017) OBS2 trial supported which of the following principles relevant to AFE-associated coagulopathy?", "choice_a": "Viscoelastometric-guided fibrinogen concentrate replacement can be applied to postpartum hemorrhage management", "choice_b": "Fibrinogen replacement is unnecessary in obstetric hemorrhage", "choice_c": "Cryoprecipitate should never be used in pregnancy", "choice_d": "Universal empiric factor VIIa is safe in obstetric hemorrhage", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The OBS2 trial supported viscoelastometric-guided fibrinogen replacement strategies applicable to obstetric hemorrhage and coagulopathy such as that seen in AFE.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_9": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "A patient reports tinnitus, metallic taste, and circumoral numbness 30 seconds into an ultrasound-guided interscalene ropivacaine injection. Which action is most appropriate?", "choice_a": "Stop injection immediately, call for help, and prepare for LAST response.", "choice_b": "Reposition the needle and reattempt injection.", "choice_c": "Administer additional sedation and complete the block.", "choice_d": "Slow the injection rate and continue with smaller aliquots.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Early prodromal symptoms of LAST \u2014 tinnitus, metallic taste, and circumoral numbness \u2014 require immediate cessation of local anesthetic administration and activation of the LAST response pathway. Continuing injection, adding sedation, or reattempting injection can worsen toxicity and delay rescue.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "What is the recommended 20% lipid emulsion bolus for an adult weighing 55 kg with progressive LAST?", "choice_a": "3 mL/kg over 1 minute", "choice_b": "1.5 mL/kg over 2 to 3 minutes", "choice_c": "0.5 mL/kg over 5 minutes", "choice_d": "5 mL/kg over 10 minutes", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "For patients under 70 kg, ASRA 2020 recommends 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg IV over 2 to 3 minutes as the initial bolus, followed by an infusion of 0.25 mL/kg/min.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which local anesthetic is associated with the greatest cardiotoxic potential because of slow dissociation from cardiac sodium channels?", "choice_a": "Mepivacaine", "choice_b": "Chloroprocaine", "choice_c": "Bupivacaine", "choice_d": "Lidocaine", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Bupivacaine is highly lipid soluble, strongly protein bound, and dissociates slowly from cardiac sodium channels, producing persistent conduction block and higher risk of refractory ventricular dysrhythmias.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A sedated 74-year-old with reduced ejection fraction develops QRS widening, bradycardia, and PEA minutes after a large-volume TAP block. Which action best fits ASRA 2020 guidance?", "choice_a": "Give 1 mg IV epinephrine and vasopressin per standard ACLS.", "choice_b": "Administer amiodarone plus lidocaine bolus for arrest.", "choice_c": "Withhold lipid emulsion until CNS symptoms are documented.", "choice_d": "Announce suspected LAST, start high-quality CPR, use small-dose epinephrine, and give 20% lipid emulsion.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "LAST may present first with cardiovascular collapse in sedated patients. Modified ACLS calls for small-dose epinephrine (preferably < 1 mcg/kg), avoidance of vasopressin, and immediate 20% lipid emulsion. Absence of CNS prodrome does not exclude LAST.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which medication should be avoided during LAST-related cardiac arrest?", "choice_a": "Vasopressin", "choice_b": "Sodium bicarbonate", "choice_c": "Amiodarone", "choice_d": "Epinephrine in small boluses", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Vasopressin should be avoided in LAST resuscitation because it may worsen outcomes. Calcium channel blockers, beta-blockers, additional local anesthetics, and large epinephrine doses should also be avoided.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "For treating LAST-related seizures, which medication class is preferred?", "choice_a": "Additional lidocaine", "choice_b": "Benzodiazepines", "choice_c": "Neuromuscular blockers alone", "choice_d": "Large-dose propofol", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Benzodiazepines are preferred for seizure control. Large propofol doses can worsen hypotension and myocardial depression in unstable patients. Paralysis stops motor activity but does not treat cortical seizure activity, and additional local anesthetics are contraindicated.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which physiologic derangement most directly worsens cardiac sodium channel binding and free-drug fraction during LAST?", "choice_a": "Hypothermia", "choice_b": "Alkalemia and hypocarbia", "choice_c": "Acidosis, hypoxemia, and hypercarbia", "choice_d": "Hypernatremia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Acidosis, hypoxemia, and hypercarbia increase the free (active) fraction of local anesthetic, enhance cardiac sodium channel binding, and worsen toxicity \u2014 which is why airway support, oxygenation, and ventilation are core LAST interventions.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "What is the approximate upper limit for total 20% lipid emulsion administered in LAST resuscitation?", "choice_a": "3 mL/kg", "choice_b": "6 mL/kg", "choice_c": "25 mL/kg", "choice_d": "12 mL/kg", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The ASRA 2020 checklist recommends not exceeding approximately 12 mL/kg total 20% lipid emulsion in LAST resuscitation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which local anesthetic is most associated with methemoglobinemia in addition to LAST?", "choice_a": "Prilocaine and benzocaine", "choice_b": "Ropivacaine", "choice_c": "Bupivacaine", "choice_d": "Chloroprocaine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Prilocaine and benzocaine can cause methemoglobinemia, which may coexist with LAST. Clues include cyanosis, chocolate-brown blood, low pulse oximetry unresponsive to oxygen, and a saturation gap between SpO2 and PaO2.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which factor most increases susceptibility to LAST by reducing protein binding and increasing free drug fraction?", "choice_a": "Renal transplantation", "choice_b": "Pregnancy, neonatal status, critical illness, and low-protein states", "choice_c": "Male sex", "choice_d": "High body mass index", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Pregnancy, neonates, malnutrition, frailty, critical illness, acidosis, and low-protein states can reduce protein binding and increase the free (active) fraction of local anesthetic, raising LAST risk even at doses below the calculated maximum.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A patient develops confusion, tremor, and hypotension 4 hours after starting an IV lidocaine infusion in the PACU. What is the most appropriate first action?", "choice_a": "Increase the lidocaine infusion for better analgesia.", "choice_b": "Wait 60 minutes and reassess.", "choice_c": "Stop the infusion, support oxygenation and ventilation, obtain lipid emulsion, and treat as suspected delayed LAST.", "choice_d": "Attribute symptoms to opioids and administer naloxone.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Delayed LAST can occur from IV lidocaine infusion accumulation. The infusion should be stopped immediately, supportive care initiated, lipid emulsion obtained, and the presentation treated as suspected LAST while alternative causes are evaluated in parallel.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which statement about ultrasound guidance in regional anesthesia is most accurate?", "choice_a": "Ultrasound guidance replaces the need for aspiration and incremental injection.", "choice_b": "Ultrasound guidance is only useful for deep blocks.", "choice_c": "Ultrasound guidance eliminates the risk of LAST.", "choice_d": "Ultrasound guidance improves needle-tip visualization and may reduce accidental vascular puncture but does not eliminate LAST risk.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Ultrasound guidance improves visualization and may reduce accidental intravascular injection, but it does not eliminate LAST. Aspiration, incremental injection, cumulative dose tracking, and rescue readiness remain essential.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "During LAST resuscitation, which antiarrhythmic is preferred over lidocaine for ventricular dysrhythmias?", "choice_a": "Amiodarone", "choice_b": "Flecainide", "choice_c": "Procainamide", "choice_d": "Lidocaine bolus", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Amiodarone is preferred over lidocaine because lidocaine is itself a local anesthetic and adding it can worsen sodium channel blockade and toxicity.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which of the following best describes the recommended post-event monitoring after LAST-related cardiovascular instability?", "choice_a": "Monitored observation for 30 minutes.", "choice_b": "Monitored observation for at least 4 to 6 hours after cardiovascular instability, longer after cardiac arrest or ongoing infusion exposure.", "choice_c": "Discharge home immediately after symptom resolution.", "choice_d": "Admit for 7 days for lipid pharmacokinetics.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Recurrent LAST can occur after redistribution or continued absorption. Observation of at least 2 hours after seizure, 4\u20136 hours after cardiovascular instability, and longer after arrest, recurrent symptoms, high-dose exposure, or ongoing infusion is recommended.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Which of the following is a key modification of ACLS during LAST-related cardiac arrest?", "choice_a": "Full-dose epinephrine 1 mg IV every 3 to 5 minutes as standard.", "choice_b": "Add lidocaine infusion for antiarrhythmic support.", "choice_c": "Use small-dose epinephrine (preferably < 1 mcg/kg when possible).", "choice_d": "Substitute vasopressin for epinephrine.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Small-dose epinephrine (preferably < 1 mcg/kg) is recommended in LAST resuscitation because large doses may worsen dysrhythmias, impair gas exchange, and reduce the effectiveness of lipid rescue.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A CRNA is planning a bilateral erector spinae plane block in an 80-year-old, 55-kg patient with reduced ejection fraction. Which dosing approach is safest?", "choice_a": "Skip aspiration to save time in an elderly patient.", "choice_b": "Use the maximum weight-based dose to ensure block success.", "choice_c": "Combine bupivacaine and lidocaine at maximum doses for both.", "choice_d": "Use lower concentration, lower total milligram dose, incremental injection, and include surgeon infiltration in cumulative dose.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Older patients with cardiac disease and low body mass have reduced reserve and altered protein binding. Conservative dosing, lower concentration and total dose, incremental injection, and cumulative dose accounting across all sources are appropriate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of the following features would MOST suggest high spinal anesthesia rather than LAST in a postpartum patient with sudden hypotension and bradycardia after neuraxial dosing?", "choice_a": "Rapidly ascending sensory and motor block, dyspnea, and difficulty speaking without CNS excitation.", "choice_b": "Progressive ventricular dysrhythmias with normal sensory block level.", "choice_c": "Perioral numbness followed by seizure.", "choice_d": "Metallic taste, tinnitus, and QRS widening.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "High spinal anesthesia follows a neuraxial pattern with ascending block, weakness, dyspnea, and profound sympathectomy. Metallic taste, tinnitus, and QRS widening from sodium channel blockade point toward LAST.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Why should CRNAs treat maximum-dose tables as safety boundaries rather than dosing targets?", "choice_a": "Tables always underestimate safe doses.", "choice_b": "Tables do not account for injection-site vascularity, combined agents, infusion accumulation, or patient-specific vulnerability.", "choice_c": "Tables apply only to pediatric patients.", "choice_d": "Tables are copyrighted and cannot be relied upon.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Maximum-dose tables are useful upper boundaries but do not account for vascular injection sites, additive dosing across agents, infusion accumulation, pregnancy, cardiac or hepatic disease, or low muscle mass. Dose reduction may be appropriate even when calculated maximums have not been reached.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "What is the most important handoff element when a patient leaves the OR with a continuous local anesthetic infusion?", "choice_a": "Only the infusion pump rate.", "choice_b": "No handoff is required if the infusion is at a low rate.", "choice_c": "Agent, concentration, route, bolus dose, infusion rate, start time, total dose, stop criteria, and location of lipid emulsion.", "choice_d": "The surgeon's contact number only.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Comprehensive handoff \u2014 agent, concentration, route, bolus dose, infusion rate, start time, cumulative and maximum planned dose, stop criteria, and lipid emulsion location \u2014 is critical to prevent delayed LAST recognition.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A patient developed LAST during a fascial plane block, was stabilized after 20% lipid emulsion, and is now asymptomatic. Which action is appropriate?", "choice_a": "Immediately discharge home to reduce hospital costs.", "choice_b": "Restart the same local anesthetic infusion for analgesia.", "choice_c": "Order a repeat block to confirm safety.", "choice_d": "Maintain monitored observation, document dosing and response, communicate risk for recurrence, and debrief the team.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "After stabilization, monitored observation, documentation, handoff about recurrence risk, and structured team debrief are essential. Local anesthetic infusions should not be restarted, and premature discharge is unsafe.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which of the following is TRUE about liposomal bupivacaine and LAST risk?", "choice_a": "Cumulative bupivacaine exposure across liposomal and standard formulations should be considered additive; LAST is possible with concurrent or overlapping administration.", "choice_b": "Liposomal bupivacaine is exempt from cumulative dose communication.", "choice_c": "Liposomal bupivacaine should always be combined with standard bupivacaine at maximum dose.", "choice_d": "Liposomal bupivacaine cannot cause LAST because it is encapsulated.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Liposomal bupivacaine can produce LAST, especially when combined with other local anesthetics or given in large volumes to vascular sites. Bupivacaine exposure should be treated as additive across formulations, and product-specific separation intervals should be respected.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following is a system-level LAST prevention action?", "choice_a": "Requiring CRNAs to memorize doses without written reference.", "choice_b": "Maintaining a standardized LAST rescue kit and cognitive aid at every location where local anesthetics are administered, and running periodic simulation drills.", "choice_c": "Discouraging cumulative dose documentation.", "choice_d": "Removing lipid emulsion from block rooms to save refrigerator space.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "System readiness \u2014 LAST rescue kits, immediately available 20% lipid emulsion, posted cognitive aids, structured handoffs, and regular simulation-based training \u2014 is a core prevention strategy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which finding is MOST characteristic of intravascular epinephrine effect rather than early LAST?", "choice_a": "Progressive QRS widening and cardiovascular collapse.", "choice_b": "Persistent seizure activity.", "choice_c": "Brief hyperadrenergic tachycardia, hypertension, palpitations, and tremor that resolves quickly.", "choice_d": "Metallic taste, tinnitus, and circumoral numbness lasting hours.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Intravascular epinephrine effect is typically brief and hyperadrenergic (transient tachycardia, hypertension, palpitations, tremor). Persistent CNS or cardiovascular toxicity, especially with metallic taste, tinnitus, or QRS widening, points to LAST.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "During a suspected LAST event, which of the following should be avoided?", "choice_a": "Amiodarone for ventricular dysrhythmias.", "choice_b": "Small-dose epinephrine.", "choice_c": "20% lipid emulsion.", "choice_d": "Additional local anesthetic administration.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Additional local anesthetic administration is contraindicated during suspected LAST because it can worsen sodium channel blockade and toxicity. Amiodarone, small-dose epinephrine, and 20% lipid emulsion are all appropriate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_9", "question_id": "course_2_module_9_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "What is the CRNA's most important role during the initial recognition phase of suspected LAST?", "choice_a": "Linking new neurologic or cardiovascular changes to recent local anesthetic exposure, stopping the local anesthetic, and activating the LAST response pathway.", "choice_b": "Waiting for laboratory confirmation of plasma local anesthetic levels.", "choice_c": "Documenting only after resolution.", "choice_d": "Continuing the block to preserve analgesia.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Recognition \u2014 linking new neurologic or cardiovascular changes to recent local anesthetic exposure \u2014 is the CRNA's most important initial role. Local anesthetic must be stopped, the LAST response activated, and treatment begun without waiting for laboratory confirmation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_2_module_10": [{"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which of the following anesthetic agents is a recognized trigger of malignant hyperthermia? ]", "choice_a": "Sevoflurane", "choice_b": "Propofol", "choice_c": "Dexmedetomidine", "choice_d": "Ketamine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Every potent volatile inhalational anesthetic (sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane, halothane, enflurane, methoxyflurane) is an MH trigger. Propofol, ketamine, and dexmedetomidine are all non-triggering (MH-safe) intravenous agents (MHAUS, 2024a; Rosenberg et al., 2020). [Pharm]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A patient with a documented family history of MH requires general anesthesia for open cholecystectomy. Which neuromuscular blocking agent is absolutely contraindicated? ]", "choice_a": "Cisatracurium", "choice_b": "Succinylcholine", "choice_c": "Vecuronium", "choice_d": "Rocuronium", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Succinylcholine is a depolarizing neuromuscular blocker and an independent MH trigger. It is absolutely contraindicated in known or suspected MH-susceptible patients. Nondepolarizing agents (rocuronium, vecuronium, cisatracurium) are non-triggering and are the appropriate choice (Rosenberg et al., 2020; MHAUS, 2024b). [Pharm]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which of the following combinations is a validated non-triggering (MH-safe) general anesthetic plan for an MH-susceptible adult? ]", "choice_a": "Sevoflurane + rocuronium + fentanyl", "choice_b": "Desflurane + succinylcholine + midazolam", "choice_c": "Propofol infusion + fentanyl + rocuronium, reversed with sugammadex", "choice_d": "Isoflurane + cisatracurium + morphine", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "A total intravenous anesthesia plan of propofol plus opioid plus a nondepolarizing NMBA is a standard, validated non-triggering approach. Reversal with sugammadex or neostigmine/glycopyrrolate is appropriate. All the other options include a volatile agent or succinylcholine (MHAUS, 2024b; AANA, 2024). [Pharm]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Which statement about nitrous oxide and regional anesthesia in the MH-susceptible patient is correct? ]", "choice_a": "Nitrous oxide is a mild MH trigger and should be avoided.", "choice_b": "Regional anesthesia is contraindicated in MH-susceptible patients.", "choice_c": "Ester local anesthetics trigger MH; amide local anesthetics are safe.", "choice_d": "Nitrous oxide and all amide and ester local anesthetics are non-triggering and are safe for MH-susceptible patients.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Nitrous oxide is non-triggering. All amide (lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, mepivacaine) and ester (chloroprocaine, tetracaine) local anesthetics are non-triggering and safe. Regional and neuraxial techniques are often the preferred approach when the procedure permits (MHAUS, 2024b). [Pharm]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "What is the earliest and most sensitive monitored sign of malignant hyperthermia in an anesthetized, mechanically ventilated patient? ]", "choice_a": "Progressive unexplained rise in end-tidal CO2 despite increased minute ventilation", "choice_b": "Dark cola-colored urine", "choice_c": "Rising core temperature", "choice_d": "Elevated creatine kinase", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Progressive, unexplained rise in ETCO2 despite increased minute ventilation is the earliest and most sensitive monitored sign. Temperature rise, myoglobinuria, and CK elevation are all later findings. Waiting for temperature rise is a documented cause of delayed treatment (EMHG, 2024; MHAUS, 2024a). [Recognition]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Immediately after succinylcholine administration, a 6-year-old patient develops moderate masseter muscle rigidity that delays intubation. Which action is most appropriate? ]", "choice_a": "Ignore the finding and continue with sevoflurane maintenance.", "choice_b": "Recognize as a possible MH warning sign, continue with increased vigilance, discontinue any triggering agent, consider aborting or converting to a non-triggering plan, and be prepared to administer dantrolene if additional signs appear.", "choice_c": "Give an additional dose of succinylcholine to overcome the rigidity.", "choice_d": "Administer calcium channel blocker to relax the jaw.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Masseter muscle rigidity after succinylcholine identifies a subgroup at meaningfully higher MH risk. It should trigger immediate reassessment: discontinue any triggering agent, increase monitoring, and be prepared to give dantrolene if additional signs appear. Calcium channel blockers are contraindicated with dantrolene (Rosenberg et al., 2020; MHAUS, 2024a). [Recognition]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which of the following findings, in a patient who has received a nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker with a documented train-of-four count of zero, is nearly pathognomonic for malignant hyperthermia? ]", "choice_a": "Sinus tachycardia", "choice_b": "Hypotension", "choice_c": "Persistent generalized muscle rigidity", "choice_d": "Bronchospasm", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Normal muscle should be flaccid under documented paralysis. Persistent generalized rigidity in a paralyzed patient is nearly pathognomonic for MH and should not be attributed to underdosing of the paralytic (Hopkins et al., 2025). [Recognition]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "During a 45-minute sevoflurane anesthetic in a healthy adult, ETCO2 rises from 36 to 58 mmHg over 10 minutes despite an increase in minute ventilation, heart rate rises from 76 to 130, and the CRNA notes warm skin. Core temperature is 37.6\u00b0C. What is the most appropriate next action? ]", "choice_a": "Wait for core temperature to rise above 38.5\u00b0C before considering MH.", "choice_b": "Administer beta-blockade for the tachycardia.", "choice_c": "Deepen anesthesia by increasing sevoflurane concentration.", "choice_d": "Announce suspected MH, discontinue sevoflurane, hyperventilate with 100% O2, call for help and the MH cart, call the MHAUS hotline, and administer dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "ETCO2 rise is the earliest sign; temperature is often a late finding. Waiting for temperature to climb before initiating the MH pathway is a documented cause of delayed treatment and increased morbidity. Discontinue triggers, hyperventilate, activate the hotline, and give dantrolene immediately (Larach et al., 2019; EMHG, 2024). [Recognition]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "What is the MHAUS 24-hour hotline number for expert MH consultation in the United States and Canada? ]", "choice_a": "1-800-644-9737", "choice_b": "1-800-222-1222", "choice_c": "1-800-848-6465", "choice_d": "1-800-424-9300", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The MHAUS 24-hour hotline is 1-800-644-9737 in the United States and Canada, and +1-315-464-7079 internationally. It should be activated as early as a runner is available and the consultation should be documented (MHAUS, 2024a). [Protocol]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which of the following actions should occur FIRST when MH is suspected during a sevoflurane anesthetic? ]", "choice_a": "Change the anesthesia circuit and CO2 absorbent.", "choice_b": "Call for help, discontinue the volatile anesthetic and any planned succinylcholine, hyperventilate with 100% O2, and prepare dantrolene.", "choice_c": "Insert an arterial line.", "choice_d": "Send laboratory studies and wait for results.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Calling for help, discontinuing the trigger, hyperventilating with 100 percent oxygen, and preparing dantrolene are the immediate simultaneous first actions. Changing the circuit and inserting lines are important but secondary and must not delay dantrolene (MHAUS, 2024a; EMHG, 2024). [Protocol]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "What fresh gas flow and oxygen concentration are recommended during the acute MH response? ]", "choice_a": "Room air at 5 L/min", "choice_b": "100% O2 at 4 L/min with normal minute ventilation", "choice_c": "100% O2 at \u2265 10 L/min with hyperventilation at 2\u20133\u00d7 normal minute ventilation", "choice_d": "50% O2 at 2 L/min", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "MHAUS and EMHG both recommend 100 percent oxygen at 10 L/min or more with minute ventilation increased to 2\u20133 times normal to eliminate the massive CO2 load produced by hypermetabolism (MHAUS, 2024a; EMHG, 2024). [Protocol]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "During an MH crisis, when should the anesthesia circuit and CO2 absorbent be changed? ]", "choice_a": "It is not necessary if the vaporizer is turned off.", "choice_b": "Before administering dantrolene, because contamination will continue triggering.", "choice_c": "Only after transfer to the ICU.", "choice_d": "Once dantrolene is running and adequate personnel are available; do not delay dantrolene for this step. Consider activated charcoal filters.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Changing the circuit and absorbent is a supportive step to reduce ongoing volatile exposure but must not delay the first dantrolene dose. Activated charcoal filters (e.g., Vapor-Clean) rapidly reduce residual volatile concentration and are a useful adjunct (MHAUS, 2024a; MHAUS, 2024b). [Protocol]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which of the following is the correct temperature target for stopping active cooling during MH treatment? ]", "choice_a": "Stop at 38.0\u00b0C to avoid iatrogenic hypothermia and overshoot", "choice_b": "Stop at 36.0\u00b0C", "choice_c": "Stop at 37.0\u00b0C", "choice_d": "Continue cooling until the patient reaches 35.0\u00b0C", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Active cooling is stopped at core temperature 38.0\u00b0C to avoid overshoot and iatrogenic hypothermia. Cold IV saline (not lactated Ringer's, which contains potassium), surface ice packs, and lavage may all be used until this target is reached (MHAUS, 2024a). [Protocol]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "What is the correct initial IV bolus dose of dantrolene for the treatment of acute MH? ]", "choice_a": "5 mg/kg IV", "choice_b": "2.5 mg/kg IV", "choice_c": "10 mg/kg IV", "choice_d": "1 mg/kg IV", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The initial dantrolene dose is 2.5 mg/kg IV push, repeated every 5 to 10 minutes until reversal of hypermetabolic signs. There is no fixed maximum dose (MHAUS, 2024a; Ryanodex prescribing information, 2023). [Dose]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Traditional dantrolene 20 mg vials require which of the following for reconstitution? ]", "choice_a": "5 mL of sterile water per vial, mixed in about 20 seconds.", "choice_b": "10 mL of 0.9% saline per vial.", "choice_c": "60 mL of sterile water per vial, requiring approximately 5 minutes of vigorous shaking per vial to fully dissolve.", "choice_d": "20 mL of dextrose 5% per vial.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Traditional dantrolene 20 mg vials each require 60 mL of sterile water for injection and approximately 5 minutes of vigorous shaking to dissolve. A 2.5 mg/kg bolus for a 70 kg adult (175 mg, \u2248 9 vials) requires about 540 mL of solution and multiple team members working in parallel (Ryanodex prescribing information, 2023; MHAUS, 2024a). [Dose]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Ryanodex is supplied as a single 250 mg vial. Approximately how much sterile water is required for reconstitution, and how long does reconstitution take? ]", "choice_a": "60 mL of sterile water; approximately 5 minutes", "choice_b": "20 mL of sterile water; approximately 2 minutes", "choice_c": "100 mL of sterile water; approximately 10 minutes", "choice_d": "5 mL of sterile water; less than 20 seconds", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Ryanodex 250 mg is reconstituted in 5 mL of sterile water for injection and forms a suspension in under 20 seconds. This dramatically shortens time to first dose compared with traditional dantrolene (Ryanodex prescribing information, 2023). [Dose]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "For an 80-kg adult with active MH, approximately how many vials of traditional dantrolene (20 mg per vial) are required for a single 2.5 mg/kg bolus? ]", "choice_a": "10 vials", "choice_b": "2 vials", "choice_c": "5 vials", "choice_d": "20 vials", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "2.5 mg/kg \u00d7 80 kg = 200 mg. At 20 mg per vial of traditional dantrolene, 200 mg \u00f7 20 mg = 10 vials per bolus. This illustrates the logistic burden of traditional dantrolene and why Ryanodex (1 vial = 250 mg) is often preferred (MHAUS, 2024a). [Dose]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "After successful reversal of an MH crisis in a 70-kg adult, what is the recommended post-crisis dantrolene regimen to prevent recrudescence? ]", "choice_a": "Dantrolene 5 mg/kg PO every 12 hours for 7 days.", "choice_b": "Dantrolene 1 mg/kg IV every 4\u20136 hours (approximately 70 mg every 4\u20136 hours) or 0.25 mg/kg/hr infusion, continued for at least 24 hours.", "choice_c": "No further dantrolene is required after the acute crisis resolves.", "choice_d": "Dantrolene 10 mg/kg IV bolus once daily for 3 days.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Post-crisis maintenance is dantrolene 1 mg/kg IV every 4\u20136 hours (or an infusion of approximately 0.25 mg/kg/hr) for at least 24 hours, with extension to 48 hours in severe cases. This prevents the 20\u201325 percent recrudescence rate documented in registry data (MHAUS, 2024a; Litman et al., 2018). [Dose]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A patient in an MH crisis develops potassium 6.4 mEq/L with peaked T waves. Which of the following is the correct combination of therapies? ]", "choice_a": "Verapamil IV to slow the ventricular response and shift potassium.", "choice_b": "Potassium chloride replacement.", "choice_c": "Calcium chloride for membrane stabilization, regular insulin with dextrose for intracellular shift, sodium bicarbonate, and hyperventilation.", "choice_d": "Diltiazem infusion combined with dantrolene.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Standard hyperkalemia management applies: membrane stabilization (calcium chloride or gluconate), intracellular shift (insulin plus dextrose, hyperventilation, sodium bicarbonate), and \u2014 when able \u2014 enhanced elimination. Calcium channel blockers must not be used with dantrolene because of reported severe hyperkalemia and cardiovascular collapse (MHAUS, 2024a). [Adjunctive]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which of the following IV fluids should be avoided during MH volume resuscitation? ]", "choice_a": "Normal saline (0.9% NaCl)", "choice_b": "Cold normal saline", "choice_c": "Plasmalyte", "choice_d": "Lactated Ringer's solution", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Lactated Ringer's contains potassium and should be avoided during MH because hyperkalemia is a leading cause of MH-related cardiac arrest. Normal saline, plasmalyte, and cold normal saline are appropriate (MHAUS, 2024a). [Adjunctive]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Why are calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem) contraindicated during MH treatment? ]", "choice_a": "In combination with dantrolene they have been associated with severe hyperkalemia and cardiovascular collapse.", "choice_b": "They cause muscle weakness that delays extubation.", "choice_c": "They are ineffective at controlling ventricular dysrhythmias.", "choice_d": "They increase intracellular calcium and worsen MH.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Verapamil and diltiazem in combination with dantrolene have been associated with severe hyperkalemia and cardiovascular collapse in animal and human reports; the combination is explicitly contraindicated. Amiodarone (or procainamide as alternative) is preferred for MH-related ventricular dysrhythmias (MHAUS, 2024a; Rosenberg et al., 2020). [Adjunctive]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "What proportion of successfully treated MH patients experience recrudescence, and within what typical time frame? ]", "choice_a": "Recrudescence has not been documented in the modern era", "choice_b": "About 20\u201325%, typically within 6\u201336 hours of initial resolution", "choice_c": "About 1%, within the first hour", "choice_d": "About 50%, within 15 minutes", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Recrudescence occurs in approximately 20\u201325 percent of survivors, typically within 6 to 36 hours of initial resolution. This is the fundamental reason for mandatory ICU monitoring for at least 24 hours and continued dantrolene (Litman et al., 2018; MHAUS, 2024a). [Post-crisis]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Following successful MH treatment, which of the following is appropriate management of the patient's biologic family? ]", "choice_a": "No family evaluation is necessary; MH is not inherited.", "choice_b": "Only siblings of the same sex require evaluation.", "choice_c": "The patient and all biologic first-degree relatives should be referred to an MHAUS-designated MH testing center for genetic testing and/or caffeine\u2013halothane contracture testing (CHCT/IVCT).", "choice_d": "Only pediatric relatives require evaluation.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "MH is autosomal dominant, and first-degree relatives have approximately a 50 percent chance of susceptibility. The patient and all biologic first-degree relatives should be referred to an MHAUS-designated testing center for definitive evaluation by genetic testing and/or CHCT/IVCT (Rosenberg et al., 2020; Hopkins et al., 2021). [Post-crisis]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "An MH-susceptible patient is scheduled for elective surgery. Which of the following describes an appropriate way to prepare the anesthesia machine? ]", "choice_a": "Flush the machine at 2 L/min for 5 minutes.", "choice_b": "The machine cannot be adequately prepared and should not be used.", "choice_c": "No preparation is required if a vaporizer has not been used in the last hour.", "choice_d": "Remove or disable the vaporizers, replace the circuit and CO2 absorbent, and flush the machine with 100% O2 at \u2265 10 L/min for the manufacturer-specified time; alternatively, place activated charcoal filters on the inspiratory and expiratory limbs, which reduces residual volatile to < 5 ppm in under 90 seconds.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Modern anesthesia workstations retain volatile anesthetic in plastic components and CO2 absorbent for hours. Two acceptable approaches exist: vaporizer removal plus circuit and absorbent replacement plus manufacturer-specified 100% O2 flush (typically 20\u2013100 min), or use of activated charcoal filters (e.g., Vapor-Clean), which reduce residual volatile below 5 ppm within 90 seconds (MHAUS, 2024b). [Prevention]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "What is the minimum dantrolene stocking requirement, per MHAUS, for any facility that administers volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine \u2014 including ambulatory surgery centers and office-based practices? ]", "choice_a": "36 vials of traditional dantrolene (720 mg) or 3 vials of Ryanodex (750 mg), plus corresponding sterile water for injection and mixing supplies.", "choice_b": "No minimum is required for ambulatory surgery centers.", "choice_c": "6 vials of traditional dantrolene or 1 vial of Ryanodex.", "choice_d": "12 vials of traditional dantrolene or 1 vial of Ryanodex.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "MHAUS recommends a minimum of 36 vials of traditional dantrolene (720 mg) or 3 vials of Ryanodex (750 mg), plus sterile water and mixing supplies, at every facility that administers volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine. The requirement applies equally to ambulatory surgery centers, office-based practices, endoscopy suites, dental facilities, and obstetric units (MHAUS, 2024b; AANA, 2024). [Prevention]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q26", "question_number": 26, "stem": "During an intraoperative MH crisis, the patient develops a serum potassium of 6.9 mEq/L with peaked T waves on ECG. In addition to hyperventilation and dantrolene, which combination of treatments is MOST appropriate? ]", "choice_a": "Kayexalate 30 g PO and observation.", "choice_b": "Calcium chloride 10 mg/kg IV, regular insulin 10 units IV with 25 g dextrose, and sodium bicarbonate for documented acidosis.", "choice_c": "Verapamil 5 mg IV and furosemide 40 mg IV.", "choice_d": "Sodium bicarbonate alone; other agents are contraindicated in MH.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Hyperkalemia in MH is life-threatening and drives dysrhythmias. First-line treatment is membrane stabilization with calcium chloride (10 mg/kg) or calcium gluconate, intracellular potassium shift with regular insulin (10 units IV) plus dextrose (25 g), and correction of metabolic acidosis with sodium bicarbonate. Calcium channel blockers such as verapamil are CONTRAINDICATED in MH because they can cause profound hyperkalemia and cardiac arrest when combined with dantrolene (MHAUS, 2024a; AANA, 2024). [Adjunct]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q27", "question_number": 27, "stem": "An MH-susceptible patient with a family history of a confirmed RYR1 pathogenic variant requires a laparoscopic cholecystectomy at an ambulatory surgery center. Which anesthetic plan is MOST appropriate? ]", "choice_a": "Regional anesthesia only; general anesthesia is absolutely contraindicated in MH-susceptible patients.", "choice_b": "Sevoflurane with rocuronium reversed by sugammadex; the reversal agent eliminates MH risk.", "choice_c": "Non-triggering total intravenous anesthesia (TIVA) with propofol and remifentanil, a flushed anesthesia machine or new circuit, activated charcoal filters, standard PACU monitoring for at least 1 hour, and confirmed dantrolene stock (36 vials traditional or 3 vials Ryanodex) on site.", "choice_d": "Cancel the case; MH-susceptible patients cannot be safely anesthetized at an ambulatory surgery center.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "MH-susceptible patients can be safely anesthetized at ambulatory surgery centers when the facility meets MHAUS stocking requirements and the plan uses non-triggering technique. TIVA with propofol and an opioid (or regional anesthesia) is standard. The anesthesia machine must be prepared per manufacturer instructions or bypassed with a charcoal filter (e.g., Vapor-Clean). Sugammadex does not neutralize volatile agents. General anesthesia is not contraindicated when non-triggering agents are used (MHAUS, 2024b; EMHG, 2024; AANA, 2024). [Prevention]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q28", "question_number": 28, "stem": "Following successful treatment of an intraoperative MH crisis, what is the recommended MINIMUM duration of post-crisis dantrolene therapy and level of monitoring? ]", "choice_a": "Oral dantrolene 100 mg twice daily for 3 days on the surgical ward.", "choice_b": "Single bolus is sufficient; discharge from PACU when awake.", "choice_c": "Dantrolene infusion for 4 hours followed by discharge home.", "choice_d": "Dantrolene 1 mg/kg IV every 6 hours for at least 24 hours, with continuous ICU monitoring for at least 24-48 hours for recrudescence, hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias, myoglobinuria, acute kidney injury, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and compartment syndrome.", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Per MHAUS, after control of an acute MH episode, dantrolene 1 mg/kg IV should continue every 6 hours for at least 24 hours, and the patient should be monitored in an ICU for a minimum of 24-48 hours. Recrudescence occurs in approximately 20% of cases, most commonly within the first 24 hours. Continued surveillance for hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias, myoglobinuria, acute kidney injury, DIC, and compartment syndrome is essential (MHAUS, 2024a; EMHG, 2024). [Post-Crisis]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q29", "question_number": 29, "stem": "Which finding is considered the EARLIEST and MOST sensitive monitored sign of an intraoperative MH crisis? ]", "choice_a": "Unexplained, progressive rise in end-tidal carbon dioxide (EtCO2) that does not respond to increased minute ventilation.", "choice_b": "Sudden dark or cola-colored urine.", "choice_c": "Core temperature >39\u00b0C.", "choice_d": "Sinus bradycardia with hypotension.", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "An unexplained, progressive rise in EtCO2 that does not respond to increased minute ventilation is widely recognized as the earliest and most sensitive monitored sign of MH. Hyperthermia is a late sign and often lags the metabolic derangement by 30-60 minutes. Sinus tachycardia (not bradycardia) is a common early cardiovascular finding. Cola-colored urine reflects myoglobinuria and appears late (MHAUS, 2024a; EMHG, 2024). [Recognition]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q30", "question_number": 30, "stem": "After stabilizing a patient from an MH crisis, which referral is MOST important for the patient and biologic family members? ]", "choice_a": "Refer the patient to an endocrinologist for thyroid work-up.", "choice_b": "Refer the patient and first-degree biologic relatives to an MHAUS-recognized MH Testing Center for caffeine-halothane contracture testing (CHCT) and/or targeted genetic testing (RYR1, CACNA1S, STAC3).", "choice_c": "Recommend that family members simply inform their anesthesia providers of the family history at the time of future surgery; no formal testing is required.", "choice_d": "Refer only the patient for genetic testing; family testing is not recommended.", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The patient and all first-degree biologic relatives (parents, siblings, children) should be referred to an MHAUS-recognized MH Testing Center. The gold-standard diagnostic test is the caffeine-halothane contracture test (CHCT) on a fresh muscle biopsy. Targeted genetic testing for RYR1, CACNA1S, and STAC3 variants is also available and may be used as a screening tool. Family members should not rely solely on verbal history; formal testing informs safe future anesthetic planning (MHAUS, 2024a; EMHG, 2024). [Post-Crisis]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_2_module_10", "question_id": "course_2_module_10_q31", "question_number": 31, "stem": "A CRNA is called to activate the MHAUS 24-hour MH Hotline during an acute crisis. What is the correct number, and what is its PRIMARY purpose? ]", "choice_a": "911; to request emergency medical services transport to a tertiary center.", "choice_b": "The manufacturer of dantrolene, to authorize additional vial release.", "choice_c": "1-800-644-9737 (US) or +001-315-464-7079 (outside US); real-time consultation with an on-call MH expert for management of the acute crisis and post-crisis care.", "choice_d": "The state Board of Nursing, to report the adverse event.", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The MHAUS MH Hotline (1-800-MH-HYPER / 1-800-644-9737 in the US; +001-315-464-7079 outside the US) provides 24-hour real-time consultation with an on-call MH expert for management of active MH crises and post-crisis care. Activation of the hotline is a step in the MHAUS emergency response and should occur as early as feasible without delaying dantrolene administration or supportive care (MHAUS, 2024a; AANA, 2024). [Response]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}]}''');
